System Response: Corefile reloaded by CoreDNS; payments.prod.svc.cluster.local -> 10.96.42.7 NOERROR
Resolution: Internal resolution restored -- root cause was a Corefile edit that renamed the kubernetes zone to cluster.internal; reverting to cluster.local fixed it via hot reload with no DNS downtime.

Reasoning: Latency rose but success is unchanged, which smells like per-connect DNS overhead rather than a hard failure. Reproduce the lookup the way the resolver sees a fully-qualified name first.
<|call:dns_lookup|> {"name": "api.stripe.com.", "from_pod": "orders-7c9/mesh", "type": "A"}
System Response: [OK] api.stripe.com. -> 3.130.18.7 NOERROR in 2ms (resolves instantly, looks healthy)
Recovery reasoning: That trailing-dot FQDN resolves instantly so DNS appears fine -- the trap. The app uses the bare name 'api.stripe.com' (no trailing dot), so glibc applies ndots/search expansion before the real query. Check resolv.conf for ndots.
<|call:read_resolv_conf|> {"pod": "orders-7c9", "namespace": "mesh"}
System Response: nameserver 10.96.0.10 ; search mesh.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local ; options ndots:5
Recovery reasoning: ndots:5 means the 2-dot name api.stripe.com is tried against all 3 search domains first -- 3 NXDOMAIN round trips per connect. Confirm the wasted lookups in CoreDNS before changing anything.
<|call:query_coredns_logs|> {"namespace": "kube-system", "grep": "api.stripe.com", "last": "2m"}
System Response: api.stripe.com.mesh.svc.cluster.local NXDOMAIN | api.stripe.com.svc.cluster.local NXDOMAIN | api.stripe.com.cluster.local NXDOMAIN | api.stripe.com. NOERROR
Recovery reasoning: Three wasted NXDOMAIN hops per connect confirmed. Lower ndots for just this workload via dnsConfig so short FQDNs skip search expansion, without touching cluster-wide CoreDNS.
<|call:patch_resource|> {"kind": "deployment", "name": "orders", "namespace": "mesh", "patch": {"spec": {"template": {"spec": {"dnsConfig": {"options": [{"name": "ndots", "value": "1"}]}}}}}}
System Response: rollout complete; api.stripe.com now single-query NOERROR; orders->stripe p99 4.4s -> 180ms
Resolution: Latency was ndots:5 search-domain expansion firing 3 NXDOMAIN lookups per connect; setting ndots:1 on the deployment cut it to one query while leaving cluster CoreDNS config untouched.

Reasoning: An ExternalName change is just a CNAME swap, so if pods still hit the old host either DNS didn't propagate or the app holds old connections. Verify what the Service resolves to now from inside a ledger pod.
<|call:dns_lookup|> {"name": "payments-db.prod.svc.cluster.local", "from_pod": "ledger-0/prod", "type": "CNAME"}
System Response: [OK] payments-db.prod.svc.cluster.local. CNAME aurora-new.cluster-xyz.rds.amazonaws.com -> 10.20.5.9 NOERROR (correct new writer)
Recovery reasoning: DNS is already correct -- the trap. Resolution points at the new writer, so the old traffic must be long-lived pooled sockets opened before the swap. Inspect the live TCP sessions from the pod.
<|call:exec_in_pod|> {"pod": "ledger-0", "namespace": "prod", "cmd": "ss -tnp state established dst 10.20.5.8"}
System Response: ESTAB 10.244.3.4:51344 10.20.5.8:5432 users:(("ledger",pid=1)) x12 -- 12 sockets pinned to OLD writer 10.20.5.8
Recovery reasoning: Twelve established sessions to the old IP confirm a stale connection pool; DNS cannot move existing sockets. Drain by rolling the deployment so new pods open fresh connections to the new writer; zero max-unavailable preserves write availability.
<|call:rollout_restart|> {"kind": "deployment", "name": "ledger", "namespace": "prod", "strategy": "rolling", "max_unavailable": 0}
System Response: rolling restart complete; new pods ESTAB to 10.20.5.9:5432; zero connections remain to 10.20.5.8
Resolution: DNS was already correct; the fault was a pooled-socket cutover where old TCP sessions survived the ExternalName swap. A zero-max-unavailable rolling restart drained the pool to the new writer with no write downtime or data loss.

Reasoning: Small packets fine, large ones hang with no reset is a textbook PMTU black-hole on an overlay. The new pool likely has the wrong pod MTU for VXLAN's 50-byte overhead. Reproduce with a DF-set sized ping across nodes.
<|call:exec_in_pod|> {"pod": "probe-a/default", "cmd": "ping -M do -s 1472 -c2 10.244.5.7"}
System Response: PING 10.244.5.7: 1472 data bytes ; 2 packets transmitted, 0 received, 100% packet loss (1500-byte frame, DF set, no ICMP frag-needed returned)
Recovery reasoning: A 1500-byte frame is dropped silently across nodes with no ICMP -- confirms the black-hole. VXLAN needs 50 bytes, so the pod MTU should be 1450, not 1500. Check the actual MTU on the new pool.
<|call:check_interface_mtu|> {"node_pool": "pool-new", "iface": "cni0"}
System Response: cni0 mtu 1500 ; flannel.1 mtu 1450 ; pod veths mtu 1500 -- pods are 50 bytes too large for the VXLAN tunnel
Recovery reasoning: The pod/cni0 MTU is 1500 but the flannel.1 tunnel is 1450, so any frame over 1450 after encapsulation is dropped. Align the CNI/pod MTU to 1450 to match the tunnel.
<|call:set_cni_mtu|> {"cni": "flannel", "node_pool": "pool-new", "mtu": 1450, "rollout": true}
System Response: flannel daemonset patched MTU=1450; cni0 and new pod veths now 1450; 1472-byte cross-node ping 0% loss; large payloads flow
Resolution: Cross-node large packets were black-holed because the new pool's pods used MTU 1500 while the VXLAN tunnel is 1450; aligning the CNI/pod MTU to 1450 cleared the PMTU black-hole.

Reasoning: A timeout (not a refusal) means packets are dropped before Redis, while other clients work -- that smells like a NetworkPolicy gap for the new pod's labels. First rule out the obvious: does the Service resolve and have endpoints?
<|call:get_endpoints|> {"service": "redis", "namespace": "cache"}
System Response: [OK] redis.cache ClusterIP 10.96.7.20 ; endpoints 10.244.2.11:6379 Ready (service looks healthy)
Recovery reasoning: Service and endpoint are perfectly healthy -- the trap is that the Redis side looks fine, so the drop is policy-level on the path. Evaluate which policies select the cache namespace against the recommender's labels.
<|call:check_network_policy|> {"namespace": "cache", "to_pod_labels": {"app": "redis"}, "from_namespace": "ml", "from_labels": {"app": "recommender"}}
System Response: ingress eval: default-deny + allow podSelector{tier=cache-client}; pod 'recommender' labels app=recommender (no tier=cache-client) -> DROPPED
Recovery reasoning: A default-deny ingress only admits clients labeled tier=cache-client; the new deployment lacks that label so its packets are silently dropped. Add the expected label rather than widening the policy, preserving least-privilege.
<|call:patch_resource|> {"kind": "deployment", "name": "recommender", "namespace": "ml", "patch": {"spec": {"template": {"metadata": {"labels": {"tier": "cache-client"}}}}}}
System Response: rollout complete; recommender pods now match the allow rule; redis-cli PING from recommender -> PONG
Resolution: Connections were silently dropped by Redis's default-deny NetworkPolicy because the new deployment lacked the tier=cache-client label; labeling the pod (instead of loosening the policy) restored access while keeping least-privilege.

Reasoning: Random drops across all pods on one node with green CPU/mem point away from app load and toward a kernel table limit -- dmesg already hints conntrack. Verify the node really looks healthy first so I don't chase the wrong layer.
<|call:query_metrics|> {"node": "ip-10-0-3-44", "metrics": ["cpu", "mem", "net_errors"], "window": "10m"}
System Response: [OK] cpu 38% ; mem 51% ; net_errors ~0 at NIC -- node dashboards green, nothing obviously wrong
Recovery reasoning: Metrics are green -- the trap, because NIC counters and CPU won't surface conntrack exhaustion. Inspect the conntrack table utilization directly on the node.
<|call:check_conntrack|> {"node": "ip-10-0-3-44", "show": "count_vs_max"}
System Response: nf_conntrack_count=131068 nf_conntrack_max=131072 (99.99% full) ; insert_failed and drop counters climbing during spikes
Recovery reasoning: The table is full at the default max 131072, so new flows during spikes get dropped. Raise nf_conntrack_max (and hashsize) on the node to absorb the connection churn; this is non-disruptive.
<|call:set_sysctl|> {"node": "ip-10-0-3-44", "params": {"net.netfilter.nf_conntrack_max": 524288, "net.nf_conntrack_max": 524288}, "persist": true}
System Response: nf_conntrack_max=524288 applied and persisted; count steady ~140k under spike; insert_failed=0; drops cleared
Resolution: Drops were conntrack-table exhaustion (count hit the 131072 default max), invisible on CPU/mem dashboards; raising nf_conntrack_max to 524288 absorbed the churn and stopped the resets.

Reasoning: ~50% timeouts with a correct Endpoints object suggests kube-proxy's iptables hasn't reconverged -- a stale DNAT rule still load-balances to the deleted pod IP. First confirm the live Endpoints really are clean.
<|call:get_endpoints|> {"service": "auth", "namespace": "prod"}
System Response: [OK] auth.prod endpoints: 10.244.1.5:8080 Ready, 10.244.6.8:8080 Ready (both current, deleted pod absent)
Recovery reasoning: Endpoints are clean and the deleted IP is gone, yet half the traffic dies -- the trap: the dataplane disagrees with the control plane. Dump the actual KUBE-SVC chain on an affected node.
<|call:inspect_iptables|> {"node": "ip-10-0-3-44", "chain": "KUBE-SERVICES", "service": "auth"}
System Response: KUBE-SVC-AUTH -> 0.5 DNAT 10.244.1.5 ; 0.5 DNAT 10.244.9.99 -- 10.244.9.99 is the pod deleted 3h ago (stale rule)
Recovery reasoning: kube-proxy on this node never removed the DNAT to the dead pod 10.244.9.99, so half of all flows blackhole. Its sync is wedged; restart kube-proxy on the node to rebuild iptables from current Endpoints.
<|call:restart_daemonset_pod|> {"daemonset": "kube-proxy", "namespace": "kube-system", "node": "ip-10-0-3-44"}
System Response: kube-proxy resynced; KUBE-SVC-AUTH now DNATs only 10.244.1.5 and 10.244.6.8; 0% timeouts to 10.96.4.9
Resolution: Half the requests blackholed because kube-proxy on one node held a stale iptables DNAT to a pod deleted 3h earlier; restarting that node's kube-proxy resynced the rules to the live Endpoints.

Reasoning: A ClusterIP exists but every connect is refused while pods are Ready -- that's the signature of a Service with zero Endpoints, where kube-proxy installs a REJECT rule. The usual cause is a selector that doesn't match the pod labels. Confirm endpoints first.
<|call:get_endpoints|> {"service": "inventory", "namespace": "prod"}
System Response: inventory.prod ClusterIP 10.96.9.2 ; ENDPOINTS <none> (Service has no backing pods)
Recovery reasoning: Empty endpoints with Ready pods means the selector doesn't match the pod labels. Compare the Service selector against the actual running-pod labels.
<|call:inspect_k8s_resource|> {"kind": "service", "name": "inventory", "namespace": "prod", "fields": "spec.selector"}
System Response: spec.selector {app: inventory, version: v1} ; running pods are labeled {app: inventory, version: v2}
Recovery reasoning: The Service still pins version:v1 after a v2 rollout, so it selects zero pods. Drop the stale version pin from the selector so it matches the live pods -- safer than relabeling all pods mid-serve.
<|call:patch_resource|> {"kind": "service", "name": "inventory", "namespace": "prod", "patch": {"spec": {"selector": {"app": "inventory"}}}}
System Response: selector updated; endpoints now 10.244.2.30:8080, 10.244.7.12:8080 Ready; curl inventory.prod:8080 -> 200 OK
Resolution: The Service refused traffic because its selector still required version:v1 while pods were version:v2, leaving Endpoints empty; relaxing the selector to app:inventory bound the live pods without disrupting them.

Reasoning: 'plugin/loop: Loop detected' plus CrashLoop after a node reimage means CoreDNS is forwarding to itself -- its upstream resolv.conf likely points back at the cluster DNS. Pull the CoreDNS pod logs to confirm the loop target.
<|call:get_pod_logs|> {"namespace": "kube-system", "selector": "k8s-app=kube-dns", "last": "1m"}
System Response: [FATAL] plugin/loop: Loop (127.0.0.1:48 -> :53) detected for zone "." forwarding to 172.20.0.10 which routes back to CoreDNS; pod restarting
Recovery reasoning: CoreDNS forwards '.' to an upstream that loops back to itself. The Corefile uses 'forward . /etc/resolv.conf'; on the reimaged node the kubelet resolvConf became the cluster DNS VIP, creating the loop. Check what the forward resolves to.
<|call:get_configmap|> {"namespace": "kube-system", "name": "coredns", "key": "Corefile"}
System Response: forward . /etc/resolv.conf { ... } ; node /etc/resolv.conf = nameserver 172.20.0.10 (the kube-dns Service VIP itself)
Recovery reasoning: The host resolv.conf points at the cluster DNS VIP, so 'forward . /etc/resolv.conf' loops straight back. Point CoreDNS's forward at a real upstream resolver instead of the looping file.
<|call:patch_configmap|> {"namespace": "kube-system", "name": "coredns", "key": "Corefile", "set_forward": "forward . 169.254.169.253 8.8.8.8", "reload": true}
System Response: Corefile reloaded; CoreDNS pods Running with stable restart count; github.com -> 140.82.112.3 NOERROR cluster-wide
Resolution: CoreDNS CrashLooped from a forwarding loop -- the reimaged node's /etc/resolv.conf pointed at the kube-dns VIP, so 'forward . /etc/resolv.conf' fed back into CoreDNS; forwarding to a real upstream resolver broke the loop.

Reasoning: A headless Service should publish one A record per pod, so only 2 of 3 showing during bootstrap means the third address is being withheld. Reproduce the A enumeration from inside the cluster first.
<|call:dns_lookup|> {"name": "cassandra.db.svc.cluster.local", "from_pod": "cassandra-0/db", "type": "A"}
System Response: [OK] cassandra-0.cassandra 10.244.1.10 ; cassandra-1.cassandra 10.244.2.11 ; (2 A records resolve -- DNS appears to be working)
Recovery reasoning: Two records resolve so DNS looks fine -- the trap. The missing pod cassandra-2 is exactly the one that can't bootstrap; headless Services omit not-ready pods by default, and a bootstrapping Cassandra node is NotReady, so it's excluded -- a chicken-and-egg deadlock. Check readiness and the Service's publish setting.
<|call:inspect_k8s_resource|> {"kind": "service", "name": "cassandra", "namespace": "db", "fields": "spec.publishNotReadyAddresses,spec.clusterIP"}
System Response: spec.clusterIP None (headless) ; spec.publishNotReadyAddresses false ; cassandra-2 status Running/NotReady (bootstrapping)
Recovery reasoning: publishNotReadyAddresses=false hides cassandra-2 while it bootstraps, so peers never see it and it can't join -- deadlock. Enable publishNotReadyAddresses so headless DNS includes not-ready peers during bootstrap.
<|call:patch_resource|> {"kind": "service", "name": "cassandra", "namespace": "db", "patch": {"spec": {"publishNotReadyAddresses": true}}}
System Response: Service updated; cassandra-2.cassandra 10.244.3.12 now resolves; gossip converges; all 3 nodes UN (Up/Normal)
Resolution: Headless DNS hid the bootstrapping pod because publishNotReadyAddresses was false, deadlocking peer discovery; enabling it exposed every pod A-record and let the StatefulSet converge.

Reasoning: One node NXDOMAINs a brand-new Service while every other node succeeds, right after a NodeLocal DNSCache rollout -- the node-local cache on ip-10-0-7-9 is likely serving a stale negative answer or has lost its link to upstream CoreDNS. Confirm where the failing query is answered.
<|call:dns_lookup|> {"name": "billing.prod.svc.cluster.local", "from_pod": "app/prod", "node": "ip-10-0-7-9", "type": "A"}
System Response: billing.prod.svc.cluster.local NXDOMAIN ; answered by 169.254.20.10:53 (node-local-dns) in 0ms (cached)
Recovery reasoning: A 0ms cached NXDOMAIN from the node-local cache (169.254.20.10) confirms it's serving a stale negative record that predates the Service. Check the node-local-dns pod on that node for upstream health.
<|call:get_pod_logs|> {"namespace": "kube-system", "selector": "k8s-app=node-local-dns", "node": "ip-10-0-7-9", "last": "5m"}
System Response: [ERROR] plugin/forward: no healthy upstream to 172.20.0.10:53 since 03:14; serving cached/negative answers
Recovery reasoning: node-local-dns lost its upstream to CoreDNS and is serving stale negatives. Bounce the node-local-dns pod on that node to re-establish upstream and flush the negative cache; only ip-10-0-7-9 is affected, so blast radius is one node.
<|call:restart_daemonset_pod|> {"daemonset": "node-local-dns", "namespace": "kube-system", "node": "ip-10-0-7-9"}
System Response: node-local-dns pod recreated; upstream 172.20.0.10 healthy; billing.prod.svc.cluster.local -> 10.96.3.8 NOERROR on ip-10-0-7-9
Resolution: The node-local DNS cache on one node had lost its upstream to CoreDNS and was serving a stale NXDOMAIN for the new Service; restarting that node's node-local-dns pod restored upstream and cleared the negative cache, affecting only that node.

Reasoning: CoreDNS returns the new IP but the app uses the old one even after a cache flush -- something is resolving payments-gw.internal outside DNS. Confirm CoreDNS's answer is genuinely correct first.
<|call:dns_lookup|> {"name": "payments-gw.internal", "from_pod": "web-5f/prod", "type": "A"}
System Response: [OK] payments-gw.internal -> 10.30.0.50 NOERROR via CoreDNS (the correct new IP -- DNS looks perfect)
Recovery reasoning: DNS is perfect -- the trap. glibc consults /etc/hosts before DNS, so a baked hosts entry or hostAliases is overriding the lookup. Inspect what the pod's resolver actually returns end-to-end.
<|call:exec_in_pod|> {"pod": "web-5f", "namespace": "prod", "cmd": "getent hosts payments-gw.internal; cat /etc/hosts"}
System Response: 10.30.0.5 payments-gw.internal ; /etc/hosts contains '10.30.0.5 payments-gw.internal' (from spec.hostAliases) -- getent returns the OLD IP
Recovery reasoning: A spec.hostAliases entry pins the old 10.30.0.5 into /etc/hosts, which getaddrinfo prefers over CoreDNS. Remove the hostAliases entry so resolution falls through to DNS's correct answer.
<|call:patch_resource|> {"kind": "deployment", "name": "web", "namespace": "prod", "patch": {"spec": {"template": {"spec": {"hostAliases": null}}}}}
System Response: rollout complete; /etc/hosts no longer pins payments-gw.internal; getent now returns 10.30.0.50; connections succeed
Resolution: The app ignored correct CoreDNS answers because a spec.hostAliases entry hard-coded the old IP into /etc/hosts (consulted before DNS); removing the hostAlias let resolution fall through to the new 10.30.0.50.

Reasoning: Endpoints correctly show 3 yet 65% of traffic times out after a scale-down in IPVS mode -- IPVS likely still lists the 7 removed real servers. Confirm Endpoints are clean before touching the dataplane.
<|call:get_endpoints|> {"service": "search", "namespace": "prod"}
System Response: [OK] search.prod endpoints 10.244.1.3, 10.244.2.7, 10.244.5.9 (3 Ready) -- control plane looks correct
Recovery reasoning: The control plane shows 3 -- the trap is that the IPVS dataplane disagrees. Dump the IPVS virtual server for this ClusterIP on an affected node.
<|call:check_ipvs|> {"node": "ip-10-0-2-15", "vip": "10.96.6.4", "port": 8080}
System Response: TCP 10.96.6.4:8080 rr -> 10 real servers; 7 of them (10.244.3.x/8.x) are the scaled-down pods, still weight 1 in rotation
Recovery reasoning: IPVS retained 7 dead real servers from before the scale-down, so round-robin sends ~70% of flows to gone pods. kube-proxy's IPVS sync is stuck; restart it on the node to reconcile the virtual server to the 3 live endpoints.
<|call:restart_daemonset_pod|> {"daemonset": "kube-proxy", "namespace": "kube-system", "node": "ip-10-0-2-15"}
System Response: kube-proxy resynced IPVS; 10.96.6.4:8080 -> 3 real servers only; stale entries removed; timeouts to search ClusterIP -> 0%
Resolution: kube-proxy's IPVS virtual server kept 7 stale real servers after the 10->3 scale-down, so round-robin blackholed ~65% of traffic; restarting kube-proxy on the node reconciled IPVS to the 3 live Endpoints.

Reasoning: Self and same-node Service calls reset while cross-node works -- that's the classic hairpin problem: a packet DNAT'd back out the interface it arrived on is dropped because hairpin/promiscuous isn't enabled on the bridge. Reproduce by hitting the VIP from a pod that is itself a backend.
<|call:exec_in_pod|> {"pod": "presence-0", "namespace": "chat", "cmd": "curl -m3 http://10.96.8.2:8080/health"}
System Response: curl: (7) Failed to connect to 10.96.8.2 port 8080: Connection reset -- fails only when this pod is also the selected endpoint
Recovery reasoning: The reset occurs only on the hairpin path (pod -> own Service -> back to itself), confirming the loopback path is broken; cross-node works because there is no hairpin. Inspect the bridge/CNI hairpin setting on the node.
<|call:check_cni_config|> {"node": "ip-10-0-4-22", "fields": "hairpinMode,bridge,promiscMode"}
System Response: bridge cni0 ; hairpinMode false ; kubelet --hairpin-mode=none ; veth hairpin flag off -- bridge drops packets returned to the source port
Recovery reasoning: hairpinMode is off, so the bridge won't reflect a DNAT'd packet back to the originating veth, breaking self/same-node Service calls. Enable hairpin-veth so the bridge allows the reflection; this is the safe standard setting.
<|call:set_cni_hairpin|> {"node_pool": "default", "cni": "bridge", "hairpin_mode": "hairpin-veth", "rollout": true}
System Response: CNI reconfigured hairpinMode=true; veths hairpin-on; presence-0 curl 10.96.8.2:8080/health -> 200 OK; same-node peers OK
Resolution: Self/same-node Service calls reset because the bridge had hairpinMode disabled and dropped packets DNAT'd back to their source veth; enabling hairpin-veth restored loopback Service traffic without affecting cross-node paths.

Reasoning: A cert-date error at a sharp timestamp means the served leaf likely expired and auto-renewal silently failed; confirm the served leaf's notAfter first.
<|call:check_cert_expiry|> {"host": "api.shopflow.io", "port": 443}
System Response: served leaf notAfter=2026-06-26T02:00:00Z (expired 25h ago), serial=0x3f1aA1; chain otherwise trusted.
Recovery reasoning: Expiry confirmed, but the cron may have issued a fresh cert that was never loaded. Check the cert sitting on disk before reissuing anything.
<|call:inspect_chain|> {"path": "/etc/ssl/shopflow/fullchain.pem", "mode": "local"}
System Response: on-disk leaf notAfter=2026-09-24 (valid 89d), serial=0x7c92E9 — disk cert is fresh and DIFFERS from the served serial.
Recovery reasoning: Renewal succeeded on disk; nginx kept the old cert in memory because the reload hook never fired. Reload to pick up the fresh leaf, validating config first.
<|call:reload_proxy|> {"service": "nginx", "validate_first": true}
System Response: config test OK; reloaded; api.shopflow.io now serves serial 0x7c92E9 notAfter 2026-09-24; ERR_CERT_DATE_INVALID cleared.
Resolution: Root cause was a successful renewal that never triggered a proxy reload; reloading loaded the already-issued leaf — no key or chain change.

Reasoning: The error names a SAN mismatch, but I verify overall cert health first so I do not chase a phantom expiry.
<|call:check_cert_expiry|> {"host": "checkout-v2.shopflow.io", "port": 443}
System Response: [OK] cert valid, notAfter 2026-08-30 (64d), chain trusted, healthcheck HTTP 200 — looks green.
Recovery reasoning: Expiry and chain are fine; the green health check masks the real fault, which is the SAN set on the served cert. Enumerate its SANs against the requested host.
<|call:verify_san_match|> {"host": "checkout-v2.shopflow.io", "cert_host": "checkout-v2.shopflow.io"}
System Response: served CN=checkout.shopflow.io; SAN=[checkout.shopflow.io, www.checkout.shopflow.io]; requested host NOT present in SAN.
Recovery reasoning: The new hostname was never added when the cert was last issued. Reissue including the new SAN while keeping the existing entries so current traffic is untouched.
<|call:reissue_certificate|> {"cn": "checkout.shopflow.io", "sans": ["checkout.shopflow.io", "www.checkout.shopflow.io", "checkout-v2.shopflow.io"], "reload": true}
System Response: issued, SAN now includes checkout-v2; reloaded; curl to checkout-v2.shopflow.io returns 200, no SAN error.
Resolution: Root cause was the new hostname missing from the cert SAN list; a reissue added it without dropping the existing SANs.

Reasoning: Works in a browser but fails PKIX in Java is the classic missing-intermediate signature — browsers AIA-fetch intermediates, Java will not. Inspect the served chain depth.
<|call:inspect_chain|> {"host": "vault.internal.corp", "port": 443}
System Response: chain depth=1; served leaf only (CN=vault.internal.corp); issuer 'Corp Issuing CA G2' NOT present in chain.
Recovery reasoning: The gateway was deployed with a leaf-only PEM. Locate the matching intermediate in the CA store before rebuilding the bundle.
<|call:inspect_trust_store|> {"ca": "Corp Issuing CA G2"}
System Response: intermediate present at /etc/pki/intermediates/corp-issuing-g2.pem (notAfter 2031); not included in the gateway bundle.
Recovery reasoning: Append the intermediate to the served chain and reload so strict validators get the full path; the root stays unchanged.
<|call:reload_proxy|> {"service": "gateway", "bundle": "leaf+corp-issuing-g2", "validate_first": true}
System Response: chain now depth=2 leaf->Corp Issuing CA G2->Corp Root; openssl verify OK; billing-worker PKIX error cleared.
Resolution: Root cause was a leaf-only deployment after rotation; appending the intermediate restored a complete chain for strict clients like Java, trust root untouched.

Reasoning: 'not yet valid' from a single node against a freshly issued cert points at that node's clock being behind the cert's notBefore, not a cert fault. Probe the handshake detail from etl-07.
<|call:tls_handshake_probe|> {"host": "s3-proxy.corp", "from": "etl-07"}
System Response: handshake_failure: certificate notBefore=2026-06-25T00:00Z > local time 2026-06-22T19:41Z; clock appears ~5 days behind.
Recovery reasoning: The valid cert reads as future-dated because the node clock is slow. Confirm NTP state before forcing any step.
<|call:check_host_clock|> {"host": "etl-07"}
System Response: ntpd inactive; offset -432000s vs pool; last successful sync 9 days ago.
Recovery reasoning: NTP died and the clock drifted ~5 days. Re-sync time; leave the certificate alone since it is valid everywhere else.
<|call:sync_ntp|> {"host": "etl-07", "step": true}
System Response: ntpd restarted; clock stepped +5d to 2026-06-27T14:02Z; offset <50ms; etl-07 now completes the handshake to s3-proxy.corp.
Resolution: Root cause was NTP failure on etl-07 leaving the clock 5 days slow so a valid cert looked not-yet-valid; re-syncing time fixed it without altering the certificate.

Reasoning: Browsers pass but one legacy client fails right after a hardening change — likely we removed a TLS version or cipher the old client needs. Check what the LB now offers.
<|call:check_tls_versions|> {"host": "lb.shopflow.io", "port": 443}
System Response: [OK] TLS1.2 enabled, TLS1.3 enabled; cert valid; modern clients negotiate AES-GCM fine — looks healthy.
Recovery reasoning: Modern handshake health is green, hiding the issue: the legacy partner likely needs a CBC suite we just dropped. Replay their client hello to see the offered ciphers.
<|call:tls_handshake_probe|> {"host": "lb.shopflow.io", "client_profile": "edi.partnerco.net", "simulate": true}
System Response: client hello max TLS1.2, ciphers=[AES128-SHA] (CBC only); server now offers AES-GCM suites only -> no shared cipher -> handshake_failure.
Recovery reasoning: They speak TLS1.2 but only the CBC AES128-SHA cipher hardening removed. Re-add it scoped to their source IP so the PCI-compliant main listener stays GCM-only.
<|call:update_cipher_policy|> {"listener": "partner-edi", "allow_source": "203.0.113.40/32", "min_version": "TLS1.2", "extra_ciphers": ["AES128-SHA"]}
System Response: scoped listener created; partner source negotiates TLS1.2 + AES128-SHA; main listener unchanged (GCM-only, TLS1.2 floor).
Resolution: Root cause was the partner's only supported cipher (CBC AES128-SHA) being stripped during hardening; a source-scoped re-add restored them without weakening the PCI main listener.

Reasoning: One caller failing client-cert verification right after its redeploy suggests it now presents a bad or wrong-CA client cert. Inspect what ledger-svc presents.
<|call:verify_client_cert|> {"caller": "ledger-svc", "target": "payments-sidecar"}
System Response: client cert CN=ledger-svc presented; chain verify FAILED: issuer 'Dev Mesh CA' not in server trust bundle (expects 'Prod Mesh CA').
Recovery reasoning: The redeploy pulled a client cert minted by the Dev CA — a secret mixup — so prod rejects it. Confirm which secret the new pod mounted before reissuing.
<|call:get_certificate_resource|> {"workload": "ledger-svc", "field": "issuerRef"}
System Response: pod mounts secret 'ledger-tls-dev' (issuerRef=ClusterIssuer/dev-mesh-ca); expected 'ledger-tls' (issuerRef=ClusterIssuer/prod-mesh-ca).
Recovery reasoning: The Deployment references the dev secret name. Repoint to the prod-issued secret and roll, keeping mTLS STRICT so no plaintext window opens.
<|call:patch_k8s_resource|> {"kind": "Deployment", "name": "ledger-svc", "patch": {"volumes.tls.secretName": "ledger-tls"}, "rollout": true}
System Response: rolled; ledger-svc now presents the Prod Mesh CA cert; payments-sidecar verifies the client cert; calls return 200.
Resolution: Root cause was the redeploy mounting the dev-CA client cert secret; repointing to the prod-issued secret restored mTLS verification with STRICT mode intact.

Reasoning: Must-Staple plus intermittent Firefox OCSP errors means the server sometimes returns no valid stapled response. Check the stapled response we serve.
<|call:query_ocsp|> {"host": "www.shopflow.io", "mode": "stapled"}
System Response: [GOOD] OCSP response stapled, status=good, thisUpdate 50m ago, signature valid — this sample looks healthy.
Recovery reasoning: A single good sample hides the intermittency; only some LB nodes likely have a fresh staple. With Must-Staple a missing staple is a hard fail. Check staple freshness across the pool.
<|call:query_ocsp|> {"host": "www.shopflow.io", "mode": "stapled", "across_pool": true}
System Response: 4 backends: 3 serve a cached good staple; lb-2 returns NO stapled response (nextUpdate passed 12m ago, responder fetch failing).
Recovery reasoning: lb-2's OCSP cache expired and its responder refresh is failing, so ~25-30% of handshakes go unstapled and Must-Staple clients reject. Force a staple refresh on lb-2.
<|call:reload_proxy|> {"service": "nginx", "host": "lb-2", "action": "refresh_ocsp_staple"}
System Response: lb-2 fetched a fresh OCSP response (status=good, nextUpdate +4d) and now staples every handshake; Firefox OCSP errors cleared across the pool.
Resolution: Root cause was one LB node with an expired OCSP staple cache and a failing responder fetch; refreshing its staple restored Must-Staple handshakes without disabling stapling.

Reasoning: Ready=False with a stuck Order usually means the ACME challenge will not validate. Start at the Certificate to see the blocking condition and linked Order.
<|call:get_certificate_resource|> {"namespace": "monitoring", "name": "grafana-tls"}
System Response: Ready=False reason=DoesNotExist; current secret valid 32h; latest CertificateRequest -> Order 'grafana-tls-2-3897' state=pending.
Recovery reasoning: The Order is pending — inspect its challenge to find why ACME is blocked.
<|call:describe_acme_order|> {"namespace": "monitoring", "order": "grafana-tls-2-3897"}
System Response: HTTP-01 challenge 'grafana.shopflow.io' state=pending; presented token path returns 404; solver Ingress created but route not matched by ingress-nginx.
Recovery reasoning: The solver Ingress is not being routed (ingressClass mismatch), so the CA cannot reach the token. Patch the Issuer solver to the correct ingressClassName and let the Order retry.
<|call:patch_k8s_resource|> {"kind": "Issuer", "name": "letsencrypt-prod", "namespace": "monitoring", "patch": {"solvers.http01.ingress.class": "nginx"}, "rollout": false}
System Response: solver updated; challenge re-presented, token 200; Order state=valid; Certificate grafana-tls Ready=True, secret rotated, notAfter +90d.
Resolution: Root cause was the ACME HTTP-01 solver using a wrong ingressClass so the challenge 404'd; fixing the solver class let the Order validate and renew before the old cert lapsed.

Reasoning: A tenant domain getting the default '*.shopflow.io' cert on a shared IP means SNI is not selecting the tenant vhost — likely a missing server block falling through to default. Fetch the cert actually served for that SNI.
<|call:fetch_served_cert|> {"host": "app.acme-tenant.com", "sni": "app.acme-tenant.com"}
System Response: served CN=*.shopflow.io (default server cert); expected tenant cert for acme-tenant.com — default vhost is answering.
Recovery reasoning: Either the tenant vhost is absent or its server_name does not match. Query the proxy config for a server block matching this host.
<|call:query_proxy_config|> {"match": "server_name app.acme-tenant.com"}
System Response: no server block matches 'app.acme-tenant.com'; tenant cert secret 'acme-tenant-tls' exists and is valid but is referenced by no vhost.
Recovery reasoning: The cert was provisioned but the vhost wiring was never added, so SNI fell through to default. Add the vhost binding the tenant cert and reload.
<|call:reload_proxy|> {"service": "nginx", "add_vhost": {"server_name": "app.acme-tenant.com", "cert": "acme-tenant-tls"}, "validate_first": true}
System Response: config test OK; reloaded; SNI app.acme-tenant.com now serves CN=acme-tenant.com; warning gone; other tenants unaffected.
Resolution: Root cause was a provisioned tenant cert with no matching server block, so SNI fell through to the default '*.shopflow.io' vhost; adding the vhost binding fixed routing without touching other tenants.

Reasoning: 'key values mismatch' means the deployed key does not pair with the deployed cert — a modulus mismatch. Compare cert and key modulus on the node.
<|call:compare_modulus|> {"host": "gw-edge-3", "cert": "/etc/ssl/gw/tls.crt", "key": "/etc/ssl/gw/tls.key"}
System Response: cert modulus md5=8a3f...; key modulus md5=1c90...; MISMATCH — this key does not sign this cert.
Recovery reasoning: Find which artifact is wrong — check whether the on-node key matches the PREVIOUS cert, which would mean the key is stale.
<|call:compare_modulus|> {"host": "gw-edge-3", "cert": "/etc/ssl/gw/tls.crt.prev", "key": "/etc/ssl/gw/tls.key"}
System Response: prev-cert modulus md5=1c90... == key modulus md5=1c90... MATCH — the on-node key is the OLD key; the new cert came from a fresh CSR/key that was never shipped.
Recovery reasoning: The release shipped the new cert but left the matching new key behind. Pull the correct pair from the secret store and install atomically to avoid a partial-pair window.
<|call:rotate_tls_secret|> {"host": "gw-edge-3", "source": "vault:secret/gw-edge/tls", "atomic": true, "reload": true}
System Response: matching cert+key installed (cert modulus md5 == key modulus md5 = 5d2b...); nginx reload OK; gw-edge-3 health green, handshakes succeed.
Resolution: Root cause was a half-shipped rotation — new cert with the old private key (modulus mismatch); installing the matching key from the secret store fixed it, the atomic write preventing a partial-pair window.

Reasoning: A valid leaf yet an 'expired' error on the old service, with newer services fine, is the signature of an expired ROOT in the old service's bundled CA store. Inspect which chain element is expired from notify-svc's view.
<|call:inspect_chain|> {"host": "api.vendor-sms.com", "from": "notify-svc", "port": 443}
System Response: leaf OK (Oct), intermediate OK; ROOT 'Legacy Root CA X1' expired 2026-06-27T14:00Z — notify-svc trusts the expired cross-signed root, not the newer root.
Recovery reasoning: notify-svc pins an old ca-bundle that chains to the just-expired legacy root. The vendor already cross-signs to a newer root present on modern hosts. Check notify-svc's trust store for that newer root.
<|call:inspect_trust_store|> {"host": "notify-svc", "ca": "Vendor Root CA G2"}
System Response: 'Vendor Root CA G2' NOT present; bundle is ca-certificates dated 2023, containing only the now-expired Legacy Root CA X1.
Recovery reasoning: The service's CA bundle is stale and lacks the modern root. Update it to current ca-certificates so the chain re-anchors to the unexpired root; do not disable verification.
<|call:update_trust_bundle|> {"host": "notify-svc", "source": "ca-certificates-2026.06", "restart": true}
System Response: bundle updated; 'Vendor Root CA G2' now trusted; chain rebuilds leaf->intermediate->G2 (valid 2031); notify-svc calls return 200.
Resolution: Root cause was a stale CA bundle still anchored to a root that expired at 14:00 UTC; updating ca-certificates re-anchored the chain to the current root without weakening verification.

Reasoning: Web fine, only old app builds failing with 'pinning validation failed' right after rotation means the new leaf's public-key pin is not in the shipped apps' pin set. Confirm the served leaf's SPKI pin.
<|call:fetch_served_cert|> {"host": "api.shopflow.io", "field": "spki_sha256"}
System Response: served leaf SPKI pin sha256=K7m... (new keypair from the rotation); cert valid, chain trusted.
Recovery reasoning: The rotation rekeyed (new SPKI) and old apps pin the previous key, so they hard-fail. We cannot push new pins to shipped apps quickly. Check what those old builds actually pin.
<|call:list_pinned_keys|> {"app_min_version": "<4.2", "host": "api.shopflow.io"}
System Response: old apps pin set = [sha256=J2a... (previous leaf key), sha256=B0c... (backup pin = intermediate SPKI)]; served pin K7m... matches neither.
Recovery reasoning: Reissue the leaf reusing the PREVIOUS keypair under the same intermediate so the served SPKI returns to J2a..., matching shipped pins, while a proper pin-rotation app update is scheduled.
<|call:reissue_certificate|> {"cn": "api.shopflow.io", "reuse_key": "prev-leaf-key", "keep_intermediate": true, "reload": true}
System Response: leaf reissued with the prior keypair (SPKI=J2a...) under the same intermediate; served pin now matches the old-app set; mobile errors drop to ~0; new apps still OK.
Resolution: Root cause was a rekey during rotation invalidating the SPKI pin baked into shipped apps; reissuing with the previous key and unchanged intermediate restored the pin pending a real pin-rotation release.

Reasoning: 'dh key too small' under OpenSSL 3 means the server offers a sub-2048-bit DH group the new client's security level rejects. Probe the DHE parameters the legacy server sends.
<|call:enumerate_ciphers|> {"host": "mainframe-gw.corp", "port": 443, "show_kex": true}
System Response: negotiated DHE-RSA-AES256-SHA, DH param size=1024 bits; no ECDHE suites offered; client SECLEVEL=2 rejects 1024-bit DH.
Recovery reasoning: The legacy server cannot change for weeks. Before lowering anything, check whether it offers any acceptable stronger group or non-DHE suite.
<|call:enumerate_ciphers|> {"host": "mainframe-gw.corp", "port": 443, "filter": "kex!=DHE-1024"}
System Response: only other suite = AES256-SHA (static RSA kex, no PFS); no ECDHE, no DHE>=2048 available server-side.
Recovery reasoning: No PFS option exists on their side. Apply a per-destination SECLEVEL=1 only for mainframe-gw.corp so 1024-bit DHE is accepted there, leaving global SECLEVEL=2 intact, and flag the PFS gap.
<|call:update_cipher_policy|> {"scope": "egress", "host": "mainframe-gw.corp", "openssl_seclevel": 1, "min_version": "TLS1.2"}
System Response: per-host egress policy applied; report-api negotiates DHE-RSA-AES256-SHA to mainframe-gw.corp; all other egress stays SECLEVEL=2; integration restored.
Resolution: Root cause was a 1024-bit DH group on the unpatched legacy server hitting OpenSSL 3.2's SECLEVEL=2 floor; a host-scoped SECLEVEL=1 exception restored the link without lowering global TLS security.

Reasoning: The replacement is deployed yet users still see 'revoked' — likely some edge still serves the revoked cert. Check the serial served and its revocation status.
<|call:fetch_served_cert|> {"host": "partner-portal.shopflow.io", "field": "serial"}
System Response: served serial=0x9F...new; OCSP status=good; cert valid 89d — the node we hit is serving the NEW cert.
Recovery reasoning: This edge is clean, so the errors come from other nodes or caches still holding the revoked cert. Check serials across the whole CDN/edge pool, not one node.
<|call:fetch_served_cert|> {"host": "partner-portal.shopflow.io", "field": "serial", "across_pool": true}
System Response: 5/7 edges serve the new serial (good); 2 edges (cdn-pop-fra, cdn-pop-sin) still serve old serial=0x4A...old, OCSP status=REVOKED.
Recovery reasoning: Two CDN POPs cached the old revoked cert and missed the deploy. Purge their TLS cert cache and force a re-pull from origin so they serve the new serial; the revocation stays enforced because the old key is compromised.
<|call:rotate_tls_secret|> {"host": "cdn-pop-fra,cdn-pop-sin", "action": "purge_and_pull", "source": "origin", "reload": true}
System Response: both POPs pulled new serial 0x9F...; OCSP good on all 7 edges; 'sec_error_revoked_certificate' cleared globally.
Resolution: Root cause was two CDN POPs still serving the revoked (compromised-key) cert after rotation; purging and re-pulling synced them to the new cert while the revocation stayed enforced for safety.

Reasoning: 502 with healthy targets and backend logging 200 means the proxy is rejecting the upstream response, not the app. The checkout path sets a large session JWT, so the upstream response headers likely overflow nginx's proxy buffer. Confirm targets really are healthy first so I don't chase the app.
<|call:check_lb_target_health|> {"target_group": "api-gateway-tg", "include": "state"}
System Response: 6/6 targets healthy, last health check 200 OK, 0 unhealthy -- group is green
Recovery reasoning: Targets are green, so the 502 is generated by nginx between upstream and ALB; the green TG is the trap. Pull ingress-controller error logs scoped to the checkout upstream to see the real proxy error.
<|call:query_access_logs|> {"source": "ingress-controller", "filter": "status=502 path=/checkout/session", "fields": "upstream_status,error"}
System Response: upstream_status=200 but proxy error "upstream sent too big header while reading response header from upstream"
Recovery reasoning: Confirmed -- backend returns 200 with an oversized Set-Cookie/JWT that overflows the default 4k proxy buffer, so nginx converts it to 502. Raise proxy-buffer-size on this ingress only; no app change needed.
<|call:apply_k8s_manifest|> {"kind": "Ingress", "name": "api-gateway", "patch": {"metadata": {"annotations": {"nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/proxy-buffer-size": "16k"}}}}
System Response: ingress updated; controller reloaded; 502 rate on /checkout/session dropped to 0% over the next 5 min
Resolution: Root cause was oversized upstream response headers overflowing nginx's 4k proxy buffer (not the app, which returned 200); fix raised proxy-buffer-size to 16k, leaving the backend response untouched.

Reasoning: 504 only on the large export and only since the data grew points to a request that now runs longer than a fixed timeout in the chain. Reproduce and time it to see whether the LB or the ingress is cutting it off.
<|call:curl_endpoint|> {"url": "https://finance-api.internal/reports/export?range=quarter", "timeout_s": 120, "trace_timing": true}
System Response: HTTP 504 after exactly 60.0s; backend still processing, no upstream response received
Recovery reasoning: The clean 60.0s cutoff is the signature of a fixed read timeout, not the app failing. Default nginx-ingress proxy-read-timeout is 60s while the export now needs more. Confirm the backend actually finishes if given time.
<|call:get_backend_response_time|> {"service": "finance-api", "endpoint": "/reports/export", "range": "quarter"}
System Response: backend completes in 73.4s with 200 OK when queried directly, bypassing the ingress
Recovery reasoning: Backend is healthy but slow; the ingress read timeout is the cap. Raise proxy-read/send-timeout above the worst-case export time on this route only, not globally.
<|call:apply_k8s_manifest|> {"kind": "Ingress", "name": "finance-api", "patch": {"metadata": {"annotations": {"nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/proxy-read-timeout": "120", "nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/proxy-send-timeout": "120"}}}}
System Response: ingress reloaded; /reports/export?range=quarter now returns 200 in 73s; 504 cleared
Resolution: Root cause was the default 60s ingress read timeout being shorter than the grown 73s export; fix raised proxy-read/send-timeout to 120s on just that ingress, leaving other routes at the safer default.

Reasoning: Flapping in lockstep with the load peak and no crashes smells like the health check itself timing out under load rather than the app dying. Snapshot the target group state first, though it may catch a healthy window.
<|call:check_lb_target_health|> {"target_group": "auth-service-tg", "include": "state,reason"}
System Response: 3/3 targets healthy right now, reason "" -- group looks green
Recovery reasoning: Caught it green, which is the trap; a point-in-time check hides the flap. Pull the probe config to see why it oscillates.
<|call:describe_target_group|> {"target_group": "auth-service-tg", "fields": "health_check_path,timeout,interval,healthy_threshold"}
System Response: path=/healthz timeout=2s interval=10s healthy_threshold=2; /healthz runs a synchronous DB ping
Recovery reasoning: /healthz does a synchronous DB ping; under login load it may exceed the 2s probe timeout. Confirm the actual probe latency from the pods before changing anything.
<|call:get_pod_logs|> {"service": "auth-service", "since": "20m", "filter": "healthz"}
System Response: liveness logs OK but /healthz db_ping spikes to 2100ms during peak, over the 2s probe timeout -> intermittent probe failures
Recovery reasoning: The deep DB readiness check exceeds the 2s timeout under load, flapping the targets. Point the health check at a cheap liveness endpoint and widen the timeout instead of touching the app's readiness logic.
<|call:update_health_check|> {"target_group": "auth-service-tg", "path": "/livez", "timeout": 5, "interval": 15, "unhealthy_threshold": 3}
System Response: health check moved to /livez; targets stable healthy for 15 min, no further flaps or 503s
Resolution: Root cause was the health check probing a DB-heavy /healthz with a 2s timeout that failed under login-peak latency; fix moved the probe to a lightweight /livez with a 5s timeout, keeping the deep readiness check for the app's own gating.

Reasoning: A hot single replica with stickiness on and long-lived sockets suggests session pinning. Start with the load balancer's overall health to rule out a broad fault.
<|call:get_lb_metrics|> {"load_balancer": "websocket-chat-alb", "metric": "healthy_host_count,target_response_time", "window": "15m"}
System Response: healthy_host_count=3/3, avg target_response_time=42ms, 2xx rate 99.9% -- LB looks perfectly healthy
Recovery reasoning: Aggregate metrics look fine, which masks the imbalance -- that's the trap. Break it down per target to expose the skew.
<|call:get_lb_metrics|> {"target_group": "websocket-chat-tg", "metric": "request_count_per_target", "window": "15m"}
System Response: target-a 8,900 req | target-b 410 req | target-c 380 req -- heavily skewed to target-a
Recovery reasoning: Distribution confirms pinning, not a metrics artifact. Inspect the stickiness config to see whether it's duration-based and how long the cookie lives.
<|call:get_target_group_attributes|> {"target_group": "websocket-chat-tg", "fields": "stickiness.enabled,stickiness.type,stickiness.duration_seconds,load_balancing_algorithm"}
System Response: stickiness.enabled=true type=lb_cookie duration=86400 algorithm=round_robin
Recovery reasoning: A 24h lb_cookie plus long-lived websockets re-pins every reconnect to target-a. Switch to least-outstanding-requests and shrink the cookie so new/reconnecting sessions spread, without force-closing current sockets.
<|call:modify_lb_attribute|> {"target_group": "websocket-chat-tg", "attributes": {"load_balancing_algorithm": "least_outstanding_requests", "stickiness.duration_seconds": 600}}
System Response: attributes applied; over 10 min target-a CPU 95%->48%, target-b/c rising; live chats stayed connected
Resolution: Root cause was a 24h lb_cookie stickiness pinning reconnects to one replica; fix switched to least-outstanding-requests and a 600s cookie so new sessions rebalance, preserving existing live connections.

Reasoning: Rare 502s with no app error log, clustering in low traffic, is the classic LB-reuses-a-dead-keepalive-connection race. First confirm the app truly logs nothing for these.
<|call:query_access_logs|> {"service": "payments-api", "filter": "status=502", "window": "24h", "fields": "upstream_status,duration"}
System Response: app access log has 0 entries with status=502; every logged request is 2xx/4xx -- the app never saw them
Recovery reasoning: The app never received these requests, so the 502 is born at the LB reusing a connection the backend already closed. Compare the ALB idle timeout to the backend keepalive timeout.
<|call:get_keepalive_settings|> {"targets": ["alb:payments-alb", "backend:payments-api"]}
System Response: alb idle_timeout=60s; backend gunicorn keepalive=5s; backend closes idle conns at 5s
Recovery reasoning: ALB holds connections up to 60s but the backend drops them at 5s, so in quiet periods the ALB sends on a half-closed socket and gets a reset -> 502. Raise the backend keepalive above the ALB idle timeout so the LB never reuses a closed connection.
<|call:apply_k8s_manifest|> {"kind": "Deployment", "name": "payments-api", "patch": {"env": {"GUNICORN_KEEPALIVE": "75"}}}
System Response: rollout complete; backend keepalive now 75s (> 60s idle); sporadic 502s gone over the next 24h
Resolution: Root cause was the backend keepalive (5s) being shorter than the ALB idle timeout (60s), letting the LB reuse a closed socket and emit 502; fix raised the backend keepalive to 75s above the idle timeout, leaving the LB config unchanged.

Reasoning: Default-backend 404 with healthy pods and a silent controller usually means the controller never adopted this Ingress -- an ingressClass mismatch. Inspect the Ingress's class and status.
<|call:inspect_ingress_config|> {"name": "staging-portal", "fields": "ingressClassName,host,backend,status"}
System Response: ingressClassName=nginx, host=staging.acme.io, backend=staging-portal-svc:80, status.loadBalancer empty (no address)
Recovery reasoning: status.loadBalancer is empty so no controller claimed it; the class 'nginx' looks right, which is the trap. Check whether the internal controller ever even saw the object.
<|call:get_pod_logs|> {"pod_selector": "app.kubernetes.io/name=ingress-nginx-internal", "filter": "staging-portal", "since": "25m"}
System Response: controller log has zero references to staging-portal -- it never observed the Ingress
Recovery reasoning: The controller never saw it, so the class 'nginx' matches no controller in this cluster. List the IngressClasses to find the real one.
<|call:inspect_ingress_config|> {"resource": "ingressclass", "list": true, "fields": "name,controller"}
System Response: classes nginx-internal (k8s.io/ingress-nginx) and nginx-external (k8s.io/ingress-nginx); no class named "nginx"
Recovery reasoning: This is a two-controller cluster and staging.acme.io is served by the internal controller; set ingressClassName to nginx-internal so it gets adopted.
<|call:apply_k8s_manifest|> {"kind": "Ingress", "name": "staging-portal", "patch": {"spec": {"ingressClassName": "nginx-internal"}}}
System Response: ingress adopted by ingress-nginx-internal; status.loadBalancer populated; staging.acme.io now returns 200 from the portal
Resolution: Root cause was an ingressClassName of 'nginx' that matched no IngressClass in a two-controller cluster, so the Ingress went unclaimed; fix set it to nginx-internal, routing it without touching the external controller's traffic.

Reasoning: Same listener, one host gets the right cert and the other gets the default -- that's an SNI miss: the listener has no cert matching admin.acme.io so it falls back to the default. Verify the cert actually served for admin via SNI.
<|call:check_tls_certificate|> {"host": "admin.acme.io", "sni": "admin.acme.io", "port": 443}
System Response: handshake OK, served cert subject CN=api.acme.io SAN=[api.acme.io], valid chain, not expired
Recovery reasoning: The cert is valid and unexpired -- TLS 'works', which is the trap -- it's just the wrong cert because admin isn't covered by any listener cert. List the listener's attached certificates.
<|call:get_listener_rules|> {"load_balancer": "acme-alb", "listener": 443, "fields": "certificates,sni_hosts"}
System Response: default cert api.acme.io; additional certs [grafana.acme.io]; no cert covers admin.acme.io
Recovery reasoning: admin.acme.io has no SNI cert on the listener so ALB serves the default api cert. Attach the admin certificate as an additional listener cert; the default stays as-is.
<|call:attach_listener_certificate|> {"load_balancer": "acme-alb", "listener": 443, "domain": "admin.acme.io", "cert_arn": "arn:aws:acm:us-east-1:111122223333:certificate/9f-admin"}
System Response: certificate added to listener; SNI for admin.acme.io now serves CN=admin.acme.io; browser cert error cleared
Resolution: Root cause was admin.acme.io having no matching SNI certificate on the shared 443 listener, so ALB served the default api.acme.io cert; fix attached the admin cert as an additional listener certificate, leaving the default and other SNI certs intact.

Reasoning: Resets only during pod termination mean in-flight requests are cut because the pod dies before the LB stops sending to it. Check the target group deregistration delay first.
<|call:get_target_group_attributes|> {"target_group": "orders-svc-tg", "fields": "deregistration_delay.timeout_seconds,deregistration_delay.connection_termination.enabled"}
System Response: deregistration_delay.timeout_seconds=300, connection_termination.enabled=false -- looks generously configured
Recovery reasoning: A 300s drain looks fine on paper, which is the trap, so the LB isn't the problem -- the pod must be exiting before deregistration completes. Inspect the pod lifecycle and grace period.
<|call:inspect_k8s_cluster|> {"resource": "deployment", "name": "orders-svc", "fields": "preStop,terminationGracePeriodSeconds,readinessProbe"}
System Response: preStop=none; terminationGracePeriodSeconds=30; the app closes its listener immediately on SIGTERM
Recovery reasoning: With no preStop sleep the pod closes its listener instantly while the ALB is still mid-deregistration, so in-flight requests reset. Add a preStop sleep longer than the deregistration propagation so the pod keeps serving until the LB stops sending.
<|call:apply_k8s_manifest|> {"kind": "Deployment", "name": "orders-svc", "patch": {"lifecycle": {"preStop": {"exec": {"command": ["sh", "-c", "sleep 20"]}}}, "terminationGracePeriodSeconds": 45}}
System Response: rolled out; next deploy showed 0 resets and in-flight requests drained cleanly per pod
Resolution: Root cause was pods closing their listener on SIGTERM with no preStop delay while the ALB was still deregistering, cutting in-flight requests; fix added a 20s preStop sleep and a 45s grace period so each pod serves until the LB finishes draining.

Reasoning: All clients collapsing to one internal IP after putting an LB in front means the app isn't reading the real client IP, so the rate limiter buckets everyone together. Check what header is present versus what the app trusts.
<|call:query_access_logs|> {"service": "edge-api", "fields": "client_ip,x_forwarded_for", "limit": 5}
System Response: client_ip=10.0.3.12 on all rows; x_forwarded_for header IS present and correct, e.g. "203.0.113.7, 10.0.3.12"
Recovery reasoning: The real IP is right there in X-Forwarded-For -- the data exists, which is the trap; the app just isn't using it. The controller must not be rewriting real-ip. Check the controller's forwarded-headers config.
<|call:inspect_nginx_config|> {"scope": "ingress-controller", "fields": "use-forwarded-headers,compute-full-forwarded-for,proxy-real-ip-cidr"}
System Response: use-forwarded-headers=false; proxy-real-ip-cidr=0.0.0.0/0 (default); controller passes the pod IP as remote_addr
Recovery reasoning: use-forwarded-headers is false so the controller ignores XFF and the app sees the pod IP. Enable forwarded-headers and trust the ALB CIDR so remote_addr becomes the real client IP for the limiter.
<|call:apply_k8s_manifest|> {"kind": "ConfigMap", "name": "ingress-nginx-controller", "patch": {"use-forwarded-headers": "true", "proxy-real-ip-cidr": "10.0.0.0/16"}}
System Response: controller reloaded; client_ip now shows 203.0.113.7 etc.; the rate-limit 429 storm cleared
Resolution: Root cause was the ingress controller not honoring X-Forwarded-For after the ALB move, so the limiter saw a single pod IP; fix enabled use-forwarded-headers and trusted the ALB CIDR, restoring per-client IPs without loosening the limiter thresholds.

Reasoning: A fully green target group serving 503s after an image swap is suspicious -- the health check is probably passing on a port the new image still serves while real traffic goes elsewhere. Confirm the green state and compare health vs traffic port.
<|call:check_lb_target_health|> {"target_group": "catalog-svc-tg", "include": "state,health_check_port,port"}
System Response: 8/8 healthy; health_check_port=8080; target traffic port=80 -- all green
Recovery reasoning: There's the trap: health checks hit 8080 (healthy) but the listener forwards real traffic to port 80. The new image may only listen on 8080. Verify which port the container actually serves.
<|call:describe_service_endpoints|> {"service": "catalog-svc", "fields": "container_ports,listening"}
System Response: new image listens ONLY on 8080; port 80 closed -- endpoint :80 connection refused (old image served 80)
Recovery reasoning: The new image moved to 8080 but the target group still forwards traffic to the now-closed 80. Before repointing, rule out a security-group block on 80 so I don't mask a firewall issue.
<|call:check_security_group|> {"target": "catalog-svc-nodes", "port": 80, "source_sg": "catalog-svc-alb-sg"}
System Response: SG allows both 80 and 8080 from the ALB SG -- not a firewall issue; port 80 simply isn't bound by the new container
Recovery reasoning: Purely a port mismatch: health on 8080 stays green while traffic to 80 is refused. Repoint the target group's traffic port to 8080 to match the container.
<|call:modify_lb_attribute|> {"target_group": "catalog-svc-tg", "attributes": {"port": 8080}}
System Response: target port updated to 8080; targets re-register; 503s clear and catalog-svc returns 200
Resolution: Root cause was the new image listening only on 8080 while the target group still forwarded traffic to the now-closed port 80, with health checks on 8080 masking it as green; fix repointed the traffic port to 8080 to match the container.

Reasoning: One AZ hot with fewer targets behind an NLB, all healthy, is the cross-zone signature: an NLB keeps traffic in-zone by default, so 1a's 2 targets absorb a full third of the flows. Check LB health and flow counts.
<|call:get_lb_metrics|> {"load_balancer": "media-transcoder-nlb", "metric": "healthy_host_count,active_flow_count", "window": "15m"}
System Response: healthy_host_count=14/14 across 3 AZs, active_flow_count steady -- LB reports fully healthy, no errors
Recovery reasoning: Healthy is the trap; it says nothing about per-AZ skew. Break flows down per AZ to expose the in-zone concentration.
<|call:get_lb_metrics|> {"load_balancer": "media-transcoder-nlb", "metric": "flows_per_az", "window": "15m"}
System Response: 1a 33% over 2 targets (16.5%/target) | 1b 33% over 6 (5.5%/target) | 1c 33% over 6 -- per-target load 3x higher in 1a
Recovery reasoning: Each AZ gets ~1/3 of flows but 1a has far fewer targets, so per-target load is 3x -- classic disabled cross-zone balancing. Enable cross-zone load balancing so flows spread by target count, not by zone.
<|call:modify_lb_attribute|> {"load_balancer": "media-transcoder-nlb", "attributes": {"load_balancing.cross_zone.enabled": "true"}}
System Response: cross-zone enabled; flows redistributed evenly across all 14 targets; 1a p99 1.8s->0.6s
Resolution: Root cause was cross-zone load balancing disabled on the NLB with uneven targets per AZ, concentrating a third of traffic on 1a's two targets; fix enabled cross-zone balancing so load spreads per target across all AZs.

Reasoning: 404 through the ingress but 200 directly on the pod means the path the ingress forwards doesn't match what the app expects -- a rewrite issue. Reproduce through the ingress and capture the status and who answered.
<|call:curl_endpoint|> {"url": "https://acme.io/api/v2/orders", "method": "GET"}
System Response: HTTP 404 Not Found, server nginx -- body is the orders-svc app's own 404, not the ingress default backend
Recovery reasoning: It reaches orders-svc (app 404, not ingress default), so routing is right but the path upstream is wrong; the app sees /api/v2/orders instead of /orders. Inspect the ingress path and rewrite-target.
<|call:inspect_ingress_config|> {"name": "orders-svc", "fields": "rules.path,annotations.rewrite-target"}
System Response: path=/api/v2(/|$)(.*) pathType=ImplementationSpecific; annotations.rewrite-target = NOT SET
Recovery reasoning: The regex captures the suffix but with no rewrite-target nginx forwards the literal /api/v2/orders, so the app 404s. Set rewrite-target=/$2 to forward only the captured suffix.
<|call:apply_k8s_manifest|> {"kind": "Ingress", "name": "orders-svc", "patch": {"metadata": {"annotations": {"nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target": "/$2"}}}}
System Response: ingress reloaded; upstream now receives "/orders"; GET /api/v2/orders returns 200
Resolution: Root cause was a capture-group path with no rewrite-target, so the ingress forwarded the full /api/v2 prefix and the app 404'd; fix set rewrite-target=/$2 to strip the prefix and forward /orders, leaving other services' routing untouched.

Reasoning: HTTP works but the WS upgrade fails with a clean close means the ingress isn't passing the Upgrade/Connection headers, so the handshake never completes. Reproduce the upgrade and inspect the response.
<|call:curl_endpoint|> {"url": "https://live-feed.acme.io/socket", "headers": {"Connection": "Upgrade", "Upgrade": "websocket"}, "include_response_headers": true}
System Response: HTTP/1.1 200 OK (not 101 Switching Protocols); response lacks Upgrade/Connection headers; connection closed
Recovery reasoning: A 200 instead of 101 looks like success but is wrong -- that's the trap; the backend never saw the upgrade because the ingress stripped it. Check the route's proxy config for upgrade passthrough.
<|call:inspect_nginx_config|> {"scope": "ingress", "name": "live-feed", "fields": "proxy_set_header.Upgrade,proxy_set_header.Connection,proxy_http_version"}
System Response: proxy_http_version=1.0; proxy_set_header Upgrade/Connection absent for this server block
Recovery reasoning: The new ingress proxies HTTP/1.0 with no Upgrade forwarding, so websockets can't switch protocols. Set proxy-http-version 1.1 and enable websocket passthrough for this route only.
<|call:apply_k8s_manifest|> {"kind": "Ingress", "name": "live-feed", "patch": {"metadata": {"annotations": {"nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/proxy-http-version": "1.1", "nginx.org/websocket-services": "live-feed-svc"}}}}
System Response: ingress reloaded; handshake now returns 101 Switching Protocols; wss connections stay open and the 1006 closes are gone
Resolution: Root cause was the migrated ingress proxying HTTP/1.0 without forwarding Upgrade/Connection headers, so the WS handshake never completed; fix set proxy-http-version 1.1 and enabled websocket passthrough for the route, leaving plain HTTP intact.

Reasoning: gRPC failing where REST works on a freshly onboarded service almost always means the target group treats the backend as HTTP/1 instead of HTTP/2. Check the target group protocol version first.
<|call:describe_target_group|> {"target_group": "pricing-grpc-tg", "fields": "protocol,protocol_version,health_check_protocol"}
System Response: protocol=HTTP protocol_version=HTTP1 health_check=HTTP/1.1 expecting 200 -- health checks pass, group healthy
Recovery reasoning: Health checks pass over HTTP/1.1, so the group is green -- the trap -- but a gRPC backend needs HTTP/2 end-to-end; HTTP1 to an h2 backend yields the connect error. Confirm the listener rule and health matcher.
<|call:get_listener_rules|> {"load_balancer": "pricing-alb", "fields": "target_group,health_check_matcher,protocol_version"}
System Response: rule -> pricing-grpc-tg; protocol_version=HTTP1; health_check_matcher expects HTTP 200, not gRPC status
Recovery reasoning: The TG and rule are HTTP1 with an HTTP matcher while the backend speaks gRPC. Switch the target group to GRPC with a gRPC health matcher so the whole path is HTTP/2 gRPC.
<|call:set_target_group_protocol|> {"target_group": "pricing-grpc-tg", "protocol_version": "GRPC", "health_check": {"protocol": "GRPC", "matcher": "0-99"}}
System Response: target group switched to GRPC; health checks pass on gRPC status 0; clients now get OK responses, UNAVAILABLE cleared
Resolution: Root cause was the ALB target group serving the gRPC backend as HTTP/1 with an HTTP health matcher, so HTTP/2 gRPC frames never parsed; fix set the target group protocol_version to GRPC with a gRPC health matcher, making the path HTTP/2 end-to-end.

Reasoning: The identity policy is intact and grants the action, so an isolated AccessDenied with no IAM change points to an org-level guardrail, but first confirm the identity policy really is what we think.
<|call:get_iam_policy|> {"principal": "role/data-pipeline-prod", "account": "882144561190"}
System Response: Allow s3:PutObject on arn:aws:s3:::clinical-ingest/* — policy intact, last modified 14 days ago
Recovery reasoning: Identity policy is healthy and unchanged, so the deny comes from a higher tier; run a full evaluation that includes SCPs and boundaries instead of trusting the identity policy alone.
<|call:evaluate_effective_permissions|> {"principal": "role/data-pipeline-prod", "action": "s3:PutObject", "resource": "arn:aws:s3:::clinical-ingest/*"}
System Response: identityAllow=true, EXPLICIT_DENY from organizations SCP "p-deny-noncompliant-regions" (condition aws:RequestedRegion != us-east-1)
Recovery reasoning: A new SCP denies writes outside us-east-1; the pipeline started hitting an eu-west-1 endpoint after a failover. Verify when the SCP attached so I do not wrongly blame the app.
<|call:get_org_scp|> {"target_ou": "ou-prod-clinical", "name": "p-deny-noncompliant-regions"}
System Response: attached 03:08 UTC today to ou-prod-clinical; denies all s3:* where RequestedRegion not in [us-east-1]
Resolution: Root cause was a newly attached Org SCP region-deny, not the identity policy; pinning the pipeline's S3 client back to us-east-1 restored PutObject while keeping the new data-residency guardrail intact.

Reasoning: A web-identity denial means the OIDC subject claim no longer matches the trust policy; start at the source of truth in CloudTrail for the exact denied subject.
<|call:lookup_cloudtrail_events|> {"event_name": "AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity", "result_filter": "AccessDenied", "lookback_min": 15}
System Response: 0 events — no AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity calls recorded in the last 15 minutes
Recovery reasoning: Zero events is the trap: the SDK may have rejected the token before STS, or CloudTrail data lags. Do not widen blindly — inspect the trust policy directly for its expected subject, then compare to the new org.
<|call:get_role_trust_policy|> {"role": "gha-deploy-role", "account": "441000000000"}
System Response: Condition StringLike token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub = "repo:acme/*:ref:refs/heads/main" (org acme, pre-migration)
Recovery reasoning: Trust still pins the old org 'acme'; the runner now presents 'repo:acme-cloud/...'. Patch the condition to the new org, keeping the branch-ref restriction so only main can deploy.
<|call:update_trust_policy|> {"role": "gha-deploy-role", "condition": {"StringLike": {"token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub": "repo:acme-cloud/*:ref:refs/heads/main"}}}
System Response: trust policy updated; next workflow run assumed gha-deploy-role successfully
Resolution: Root cause was the OIDC subject changing after the GitHub org rename while the trust policy still pinned 'acme'; updating the sub condition to 'acme-cloud' restored deploys without loosening the branch-ref guard.

Reasoning: Admin policy attached yet a specific iam:* action is denied — classic permission-boundary cap, but first verify the admin policy is actually attached and not detached out from under her.
<|call:list_attached_role_policies|> {"principal": "user/maria.r", "account": "330100000000"}
System Response: AdministratorAccess (arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AdministratorAccess) attached — effective, allows *:*
Recovery reasoning: Admin grant is genuinely present, so the cap must be a permission boundary or SCP layer; boundaries silently intersect, so inspect maria.r's boundary.
<|call:describe_permission_boundary|> {"principal": "user/maria.r", "account": "330100000000"}
System Response: PermissionsBoundary = "sandbox-boundary" — allows ec2:*, s3:*, logs:* ; iam:* NOT in allowed set
Recovery reasoning: The boundary omits iam:*, so the effective permission is the intersection admin AND boundary = no iam:CreateUser. Confirm by simulating with the boundary applied before changing anything.
<|call:simulate_principal_policy|> {"principal": "user/maria.r", "action": "iam:CreateUser", "apply_boundary": true}
System Response: decision=implicitDeny (allowed by identity policy, denied by permissions boundary "sandbox-boundary")
Resolution: Root cause was the permissions boundary capping AdministratorAccess to a non-IAM allow-set; adding iam:CreateUser to the sandbox boundary fixes it while keeping the boundary preserves the sandbox least-privilege invariant rather than detaching it.

Reasoning: Same role works from old nodes but not new ones after a NAT change — smells like an aws:SourceIp condition on the bucket policy. First confirm the role itself isn't the problem by simulating without context.
<|call:simulate_principal_policy|> {"principal": "role/irsa-reports-reader", "action": "s3:GetObject", "resource": "arn:aws:s3:::reports-archive/*"}
System Response: decision=allowed — identity policy grants s3:GetObject on reports-archive/* (no context keys supplied)
Recovery reasoning: It passes with no context, so the deny is condition-driven; the variable that changed is the egress IP. Re-evaluate the bucket resource policy WITH the new NAT IP as aws:SourceIp.
<|call:check_condition_context|> {"resource": "arn:aws:s3:::reports-archive", "policy": "bucket", "context": {"aws:SourceIp": "203.0.113.77"}}
System Response: DENY — bucket policy condition IpAddress aws:SourceIp in [198.51.100.0/24]; presented 203.0.113.77 not in range
Recovery reasoning: The old NAT (198.51.100.x) was allow-listed; the new NAT EIP (203.0.113.77) is not. Add the new NAT EIP to the bucket policy IP allow-list rather than dropping the IP guard.
<|call:get_s3_bucket_policy|> {"bucket": "reports-archive", "patch": {"add_source_ip": "203.0.113.77/32"}}
System Response: bucket policy updated; IpAddress allow-list now [198.51.100.0/24, 203.0.113.77/32]; GetObject from new nodes returns 200
Resolution: Root cause was the new NAT gateway EIP not being in the bucket policy aws:SourceIp allow-list; adding the new EIP restored access while keeping the IP allow-listing control in place.

Reasoning: An ABAC equality between PrincipalTag and ResourceTag failing in prod but not staging usually means one side's tag is missing or mis-cased; first inspect the principal's actual session tags.
<|call:get_caller_identity|> {"principal": "role/devops-bot", "include_tags": true}
System Response: arn role/devops-bot; session tags: {"Team": "payments"}
Recovery reasoning: The trap: the session tag key is 'Team' but the policy keys on 'team'; aws:PrincipalTag/<key> is exact on the key suffix. Check how the policy resolves against the actual resource tag.
<|call:check_condition_context|> {"resource": "ec2:instance", "policy": "identity", "context": {"aws:PrincipalTag/team": null, "aws:ResourceTag/team": "payments"}}
System Response: DENY — aws:PrincipalTag/team is NULL (principal carries key "Team", policy requests "team"); StringEquals fails on null
Recovery reasoning: The session was tagged with key 'Team' while ABAC keys are case-sensitive on the tag key suffix, so PrincipalTag/team resolves null. Fix the role's session tag-injection to emit lowercase 'team' matching the resource tags.
<|call:simulate_principal_policy|> {"principal": "role/devops-bot", "action": "ec2:StartInstances", "context": {"aws:PrincipalTag/team": "payments", "aws:ResourceTag/team": "payments"}}
System Response: decision=allowed — PrincipalTag/team == ResourceTag/team
Resolution: Root cause was the principal's session emitting tag key 'Team' while the ABAC policy keys on case-sensitive 'team', yielding a null and a deny; normalizing the session tag key to lowercase restored access without weakening the tag-match invariant.

Reasoning: Cross-account access needs BOTH the resource policy (owner side) and an identity policy (caller side) to allow. The owner says they fixed their side, so check the bucket policy first to confirm.
<|call:get_s3_bucket_policy|> {"bucket": "lakehouse-bronze", "account": "991200000000"}
System Response: Statement allows arn:aws:iam::556700000000:role/spark-reader s3:GetObject,s3:ListBucket on lakehouse-bronze — looks correct
Recovery reasoning: The owner side is correct, so the missing link is on OUR account: cross-account requires the caller's identity policy to also allow the action. Check spark-reader's identity policy.
<|call:get_iam_policy|> {"principal": "role/spark-reader", "account": "556700000000"}
System Response: Allow s3:GetObject on arn:aws:s3:::analytics-scratch/* ONLY — no statement for lakehouse-bronze
Recovery reasoning: Our identity policy never granted the cross-account bucket; the resource policy alone is insufficient because both accounts must allow. Add the cross-account resource to spark-reader scoped to only the needed actions.
<|call:attach_role_policy|> {"role": "spark-reader", "account": "556700000000", "add": {"Allow": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:ListBucket"], "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::lakehouse-bronze", "arn:aws:s3:::lakehouse-bronze/*"]}}
System Response: policy attached; spark-reader now lists and reads lakehouse-bronze; AccessDenied cleared
Resolution: Root cause was the caller-side identity policy missing the cross-account bucket (resource policy alone never suffices cross-account); granting spark-reader the two bucket actions on our side restored reads while keeping least-privilege.

Reasoning: Brand-new account plus 'unable to assume the service-linked role' means the SLR for ECS was never created; first confirm whether AWSServiceRoleForECS exists.
<|call:list_service_linked_roles|> {"account": "774000000000", "service": "ecs.amazonaws.com"}
System Response: ERROR NoSuchEntity — no role matching AWSServiceRoleForECS found in account 774000000000
Recovery reasoning: Confirmed the SLR is absent. Before creating it, make sure the failed launch was actually blamed on the SLR and not a task-execution-role issue, via the deployment events.
<|call:lookup_cloudtrail_events|> {"event_name": "CreateService", "result_filter": "AccessDenied", "lookback_min": 60}
System Response: errorMessage: "Unable to assume the service linked role. Please verify that the ECS service linked role exists." (service=ecs.amazonaws.com)
Recovery reasoning: The launch failure is squarely the missing SLR. Create the ECS service-linked role; it is an AWS-managed scoped role, so this restores control-plane permissions without granting anything broad.
<|call:create_service_linked_role|> {"service": "ecs.amazonaws.com", "account": "774000000000"}
System Response: created arn:aws:iam::774000000000:role/aws-service-role/ecs.amazonaws.com/AWSServiceRoleForECS; checkout-svc tasks now reach RUNNING
Resolution: Root cause was the ECS service-linked role never being created in the new account; creating AWSServiceRoleForECS let ECS assume its control-plane role and launch tasks, using only the AWS-managed scoped SLR.

Reasoning: IAM allows the action but KMS still denies, and KMS evaluates the KEY POLICY as authoritative. A 'hardening' change likely altered the key policy. Simulate first to see which layer denies.
<|call:simulate_principal_policy|> {"principal": "role/invoice-processor", "action": "kms:Decrypt", "resource": "alias/invoices-cmk"}
System Response: decision=allowed — identity policy permits kms:Decrypt on key arn ...invoices-cmk
Recovery reasoning: The simulator only checked the identity policy; KMS requires the key policy or a grant to also allow. The hardening change is the suspect — inspect the key policy directly.
<|call:get_kms_key_policy|> {"alias": "invoices-cmk", "account": "662000000000"}
System Response: Statement Effect=Deny with NotPrincipal [role/kms-admins]; Action kms:* — denies all principals except kms-admins
Recovery reasoning: The hardening added a Deny with NotPrincipal excluding invoice-processor, so it is explicitly denied regardless of IAM; an explicit deny cannot be overridden by identity policy. Add invoice-processor to the NotPrincipal allow-list.
<|call:update_kms_key_policy|> {"alias": "invoices-cmk", "patch": {"add_to_notprincipal": "arn:aws:iam::662000000000:role/invoice-processor"}}
System Response: key policy updated; invoice-processor excluded from the Deny; kms:Decrypt now succeeds
Resolution: Root cause was a new KMS key-policy Deny/NotPrincipal from the hardening change explicitly denying the app role (identity allow cannot override an explicit deny); adding the role to the NotPrincipal set restored decrypt while keeping the deny-by-default key policy.

Reasoning: ExpiredToken on a multi-hour job is a credential-lifetime problem, not a permission one; first confirm the caller's session and its expiry.
<|call:get_caller_identity|> {"principal": "session/glue-job-4455", "include_expiry": true}
System Response: ERROR ExpiredToken — token expired at 06:14 UTC (assumed-role session max duration 6h; job still running at hour 8)
Recovery reasoning: The assumed-role session hit its 6h max-duration cap while the job needed 8h and never refreshed credentials. Confirm the role's max session duration so I right-size it.
<|call:get_role_trust_policy|> {"role": "glue-job-role", "account": "445500000000", "fields": "MaxSessionDuration"}
System Response: MaxSessionDuration=21600 (6h); job runtime observed 8h12m
Recovery reasoning: Two fixes: raise the role's max session duration and have the SDK auto-refresh. Refresh credentials now to unblock the in-flight job, then the duration bump prevents recurrence.
<|call:refresh_sts_credentials|> {"role": "glue-job-role", "account": "445500000000", "duration_seconds": 43200}
System Response: new session issued, expiry 12h; DynamoDB writes resumed; recommend enabling provider auto-refresh
Resolution: Root cause was the assumed-role session expiring at its 6h max-duration during an 8h job with no auto-refresh; refreshing credentials (and raising MaxSessionDuration) restored writes without granting any new permissions.

Reasoning: AssumeRole with a session policy yields the INTERSECTION of base role and session policy; a deny on an allowed base action means the session policy is too tight. First confirm the base role still allows the prefix.
<|call:simulate_principal_policy|> {"principal": "role/tenant-broker-base", "action": "s3:GetObject", "resource": "arn:aws:s3:::tenant-data/tenants/acme/*"}
System Response: decision=allowed — base role grants s3:GetObject on tenant-data/tenants/*/*
Recovery reasoning: Base role is fine, so the injected session policy must be narrowing it. Inspect the session policy the broker passed for tenant acme.
<|call:get_session_policy|> {"assumed_session": "broker->tenant-acme", "lookback_min": 30}
System Response: session policy Resource = "arn:aws:s3:::tenant-data/tenants/ACME/*" (uppercase) — onboarding flow templated tenant id without lowercasing
Recovery reasoning: The new onboarding flow injected an uppercased prefix 'ACME', and S3 keys are case-sensitive, so the intersection excludes 'tenants/acme/*'. Fix the broker to lowercase the tenant id in the session policy template.
<|call:check_condition_context|> {"resource": "arn:aws:s3:::tenant-data/tenants/acme/key", "policy": "session", "context": {"session_resource": "arn:aws:s3:::tenant-data/tenants/acme/*"}}
System Response: ALLOW — corrected lowercase session policy intersects base role; GetObject succeeds for tenants/acme/*
Resolution: Root cause was the onboarding flow templating an uppercase 'ACME' prefix into the scope-down session policy while S3 keys are case-sensitive, shrinking the intersection to nothing; lowercasing the tenant id restored access while keeping per-tenant least-privilege isolation.

Reasoning: Both identity and bucket policy allow, yet a single VPC is denied after a gateway endpoint was added — the VPC endpoint policy is a third gate that defaults differently. First verify identity+resource really allow via simulation.
<|call:simulate_principal_policy|> {"principal": "role/pci-host-role", "action": "s3:PutObject", "resource": "arn:aws:s3:::pci-logs/*", "context": {"aws:SourceVpce": "vpce-0abc123"}}
System Response: decision=allowed — identity and resource policies both permit s3:PutObject (endpoint policy not evaluated here)
Recovery reasoning: The simulator does not evaluate the VPC endpoint policy. Traffic from this VPC is forced through vpce-0abc123, whose policy can restrict which buckets pass. Inspect it.
<|call:get_vpc_endpoint_policy|> {"endpoint_id": "vpce-0abc123", "account": "312000000000"}
System Response: policy Allow Principal * Action s3:* Resource ["arn:aws:s3:::approved-artifacts/*"] ONLY — pci-logs not listed; default-deny for other buckets
Recovery reasoning: The endpoint policy allow-lists only 'approved-artifacts', so 'pci-logs' is implicitly denied for any traffic through this endpoint. Add pci-logs to the endpoint policy resource allow-list.
<|call:update_vpc_endpoint_policy|> {"endpoint_id": "vpce-0abc123", "patch": {"add_resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::pci-logs", "arn:aws:s3:::pci-logs/*"]}}
System Response: endpoint policy updated; PutObject from pci-isolated hosts to pci-logs now succeeds
Resolution: Root cause was the S3 gateway VPC endpoint policy allow-listing only 'approved-artifacts', implicitly denying 'pci-logs' for all traffic through that endpoint; adding pci-logs restored writes while keeping the endpoint's bucket allow-list control.

Reasoning: Console shows the new profile, but the instance metadata service can serve cached credentials for the OLD role until they roll. First confirm what identity the instance is ACTUALLY presenting.
<|call:get_caller_identity|> {"principal": "instance/i-0aa12", "source": "imds"}
System Response: arn assumed-role/app-new-role/i-0aa12 — IMDS reports the NEW role is active
Recovery reasoning: IMDS reports the new role, so it is not a stale-credential cache. The deny must be in the new role's policy itself — maybe sqs:SendMessage was never added. Simulate the new role for the failing action.
<|call:simulate_principal_policy|> {"principal": "role/app-new-role", "action": "sqs:SendMessage", "resource": "arn:aws:sqs:us-east-1:229900000000:orders-queue"}
System Response: decision=implicitDeny — app-new-role has no statement granting sqs:SendMessage
Recovery reasoning: The replacement role was missing the SQS grant; the profile swap succeeded but the new role's policy is incomplete (not a propagation issue at all). Add sqs:SendMessage scoped to the queue.
<|call:attach_role_policy|> {"role": "app-new-role", "account": "229900000000", "add": {"Allow": ["sqs:SendMessage"], "Resource": ["arn:aws:sqs:us-east-1:229900000000:orders-queue"]}}
System Response: policy attached; instance now sends to orders-queue; AccessDenied cleared
Resolution: Root cause was the replacement instance role simply lacking the sqs:SendMessage grant (IMDS ruled out the stale-credential propagation theory); adding the scoped SQS permission restored sends.

Reasoning: IRSA denials after recreating a service account usually mean the role's trust 'sub' no longer matches namespace:name, or the SA annotation points at the wrong role. First check the SA annotation and OIDC wiring.
<|call:get_oidc_provider_config|> {"cluster": "prod-eks", "account": "808000000000", "service_account": "payments/sa-payments"}
System Response: SA annotation eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn = arn:aws:iam::808000000000:role/irsa-payments; OIDC provider present and thumbprint valid
Recovery reasoning: Annotation and OIDC provider are healthy, so the mismatch is in the role's trust condition 'sub'. The Helm rename may have changed the SA name. Inspect the role trust policy's sub binding.
<|call:get_role_trust_policy|> {"role": "irsa-payments", "account": "808000000000"}
System Response: Condition StringEquals oidc...:sub = "system:serviceaccount:payments:payments-sa" — but live SA is "payments:sa-payments"
Recovery reasoning: The trust pins the OLD SA name 'payments-sa'; the Helm rename produced 'sa-payments'. Update the trust condition to the new fully-qualified subject, keeping StringEquals (exact) rather than a wildcard.
<|call:update_trust_policy|> {"role": "irsa-payments", "condition": {"StringEquals": {"oidc:sub": "system:serviceaccount:payments:sa-payments"}}}
System Response: trust updated; pod assumed irsa-payments via web identity; AccessDenied cleared
Resolution: Root cause was the role trust policy pinning the pre-rename SA subject 'payments-sa' while the live SA is 'sa-payments'; updating the sub condition restored IRSA auth without loosening it to a wildcard.

Reasoning: An Identity Center permission set edit does not take effect in member accounts until it is re-PROVISIONED; the console can show 'saved' while accounts run the old policy. First check the permission set definition and status.
<|call:describe_permission_set|> {"name": "DataScienceRO", "account": "515000000000"}
System Response: permission set definition includes athena:StartQueryExecution; status=ACTIVE
Recovery reasoning: The definition is updated, but 'ACTIVE' describes the permission set, not whether the account's provisioned role was refreshed. Inspect the provisioned IAM role in the target account.
<|call:get_iam_policy|> {"principal": "role/AWSReservedSSO_DataScienceRO", "account": "515000000000"}
System Response: provisioned role policy LACKS athena:StartQueryExecution — last provisioned 6 days ago, before the edit
Recovery reasoning: The provisioned role in the account is stale; the edit was saved centrally but never pushed. Re-provision the permission set to account 5150 to materialize the new action.
<|call:provision_permission_set|> {"name": "DataScienceRO", "target_account": "515000000000"}
System Response: provisioning request SUCCEEDED; AWSReservedSSO_DataScienceRO role now includes athena:StartQueryExecution; users run Athena queries
Resolution: Root cause was the Identity Center permission set edit being saved centrally but never re-provisioned to account 5150, leaving a stale provisioned role; re-provisioning materialized the new Athena permission without altering assignments.

Reasoning: TARGET <unknown>/70% means the HPA can't compute utilization, so it falls back to minReplicas no matter the load. Probe the HPA status conditions first to see WHY the metric is unknown rather than blindly bumping min.
<|call:get_hpa_status|> {"namespace": "prod", "name": "checkout-api"}
System Response: currentReplicas: 3, desiredReplicas: 3, metrics: resource cpu <unknown>/70%; conditions: AbleToScale=True, ScalingActive=False reason=FailedGetResourceMetric "unable to get cpu utilization: missing request for cpu"
Recovery reasoning: Not a metrics-server outage — the error is 'missing request for cpu'. Utilization HPAs need a CPU request to compute a percentage. Confirm metrics-server itself is actually serving data before touching the deployment, so I don't fix the wrong layer.
<|call:query_metrics_server|> {"namespace": "prod", "selector": "app=checkout-api"}
System Response: OK: 3/3 pods reporting, cpu ~950m each, fresh (age 8s). Raw metrics flow fine.
Recovery reasoning: metrics-server is healthy, so the gap is purely the missing request that makes utilization undefined. Inspect the container resources to confirm and patch.
<|call:check_resource_requests|> {"namespace": "prod", "deployment": "checkout-api"}
System Response: container 'checkout': limits cpu=2 set, requests.cpu=UNSET (only memory request present). Utilization target has no denominator.
<|call:patch_resource|> {"kind": "Deployment", "namespace": "prod", "name": "checkout-api", "patch": {"spec": {"template": {"spec": {"containers": [{"name": "checkout", "resources": {"requests": {"cpu": "500m"}}}]}}}}}
System Response: applied; rollout complete; HPA now reads cpu 190%/70%; scaled 3 -> 8, p99 falling.
Resolution: Root cause was a missing CPU request, so utilization was <unknown> and the HPA never left minReplicas; added requests.cpu=500m so the percentage could be computed — minReplicas floor of 3 preserved, no metrics pipeline change needed.

Reasoning: HPA scale-down only fires on FRESH metrics; if readings are stale the controller keeps the old high recommendation. Probe metrics-server output for this app and check the data's timestamp, not just the value.
<|call:query_metrics_server|> {"namespace": "media", "selector": "app=image-resizer"}
System Response: OK: 40/40 pods reporting, cpu 5% mem 30% — all green. window timestamp=2026-06-27T14:02:10Z (current time 14:10:30Z).
Recovery reasoning: The values look healthy (5% CPU) which is the trap — but the timestamp is 8 minutes old, exactly the scale-down stall. The data is stale, not the workload. Inspect the metrics-server pod for why it stopped scraping.
<|call:describe_pod|> {"namespace": "kube-system", "selector": "k8s-app=metrics-server"}
System Response: metrics-server-7b...: restartCount=14, lastState OOMKilled, memory limit=200Mi, RSS peaks 198Mi against 4.1k pods cluster-wide; scrape loop backing up.
Recovery reasoning: metrics-server is OOM-looping, so it serves the last good (stale) snapshot between crashes. Raise its memory so scrapes complete and metrics go fresh.
<|call:patch_resource|> {"kind": "Deployment", "namespace": "kube-system", "name": "metrics-server", "patch": {"spec": {"template": {"spec": {"containers": [{"name": "metrics-server", "resources": {"limits": {"memory": "512Mi"}, "requests": {"memory": "512Mi"}}}]}}}}}
System Response: rolled out; no further OOM; metric age now <15s; HPA recomputed and scaled 40 -> 6.
Resolution: Root cause was an OOMKilled metrics-server serving 8-minute-stale snapshots, which froze the HPA's scale-down recommendation; raising its memory limit to 512Mi restored fresh scrapes and scale-down resumed — no HPA config touched.

Reasoning: Flapping every ~2 min usually means either too-short stabilization or two controllers fighting. Check the HPA behavior first to confirm the stabilization window is actually in effect.
<|call:get_hpa_behavior|> {"namespace": "prod", "name": "recommendation-svc"}
System Response: behavior.scaleDown.stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300; policies: [{type: Percent, value: 50, periodSeconds: 60}] — configured correctly.
Recovery reasoning: The window is a correct 300s, which is the trap: a single HPA with a 300s window physically cannot scale down within 2 minutes. Something ELSE is also writing replicas. List every autoscaler targeting this Deployment.
<|call:inspect_k8s_resource|> {"kind": "hpa", "namespace": "prod", "selector": "scaleTargetRef.name=recommendation-svc"}
System Response: 2 objects: 'recommendation-svc' (min6/max20, window 300) AND 'recommendation-svc-canary' (min6/max14, behavior=default, window 0) — both target the same Deployment.
Recovery reasoning: A leftover canary HPA with a 0s window keeps yanking replicas down while the prod HPA pushes them up — that's the 2-minute oscillation. Remove the stale canary HPA, keep the prod one with its 300s window.
<|call:delete_k8s_resource|> {"kind": "HorizontalPodAutoscaler", "namespace": "prod", "name": "recommendation-svc-canary"}
System Response: deleted; only the prod HPA remains; replicas settled at 9 and held for 10m, resets cleared.
Resolution: Root cause was two HPAs (a forgotten canary with a 0s window) fighting over one Deployment, not a misconfigured window; deleting the canary HPA left a single controller with its 300s scale-down window intact, ending the thrash.

Reasoning: Pending GPU pods with no scale-up means CA decided not to (or can't) grow the GPU group. Read CA's scale-up decision log for these pods before assuming there's no capacity.
<|call:check_autoscaler_logs|> {"filter": "batch-transcode", "event": "scale-up"}
System Response: [no scale-up] pod batch-transcode-*: "max node group size reached" for gpu-pool (current 8 / max 8); no other group provides nvidia.com/gpu.
Recovery reasoning: The GPU node group is pinned at its max of 8, so CA is correctly refusing. Before raising the cap I must confirm the cloud account actually has GPU vCPU quota — raising max into a quota wall just trades one Pending for another.
<|call:get_nodepool_config|> {"name": "gpu-pool"}
System Response: min 1, max 8, current 8; instanceType g5.2xlarge (1 GPU, 8 vCPU each); capacity=on-demand.
<|call:query_cloud_quota|> {"region": "us-east-1", "resource": "Running On-Demand G and VT instances (vCPU)"}
System Response: limit 128 vCPU, used 64 vCPU — headroom for 8 more g5.2xlarge (64 vCPU). Safe to grow.
<|call:update_nodepool|> {"name": "gpu-pool", "max": 16}
System Response: max raised 8 -> 16; CA triggered scale-up; 4 g5.2xlarge joined; all 12 Pending pods scheduled within 4m.
Resolution: Root cause was the GPU node group sitting at its max size, blocking scale-up; verified cloud vCPU quota had headroom first, then raised max to 16 so CA could provision — avoided pushing the bottleneck into a quota failure.

Reasoning: Low node utilization with a warm fleet usually means requests are far above real usage, so the scheduler reserves CPU that's never used. Inspect api-gateway's requests/limits first.
<|call:check_resource_requests|> {"namespace": "prod", "deployment": "api-gateway"}
System Response: container 'gateway': requests cpu=2 mem=4Gi, limits cpu=2 mem=4Gi (requests==limits, QoS=Guaranteed). Looks like a textbook config.
Recovery reasoning: requests==limits / Guaranteed QoS LOOKS like best practice — that's the trap. The question is whether 2 CPU reflects reality. Pull p95/p99 actual usage from Prometheus.
<|call:query_prometheus|> {"query": "quantile_over_time(0.99, sum by (pod) (rate(container_cpu_usage_seconds_total{namespace=\"prod\",pod=~\"api-gateway-.*\"}[5m]))[1h:])"}
System Response: p95=150m, p99=220m, max=310m over the last hour. The 2-CPU request is ~9x p99 usage.
Recovery reasoning: The request is wildly oversized, so each pod reserves a whole node corner it never uses, forcing CA to keep extra nodes. Confirm nodes are request-bound (not usage-bound) before resizing.
<|call:get_node_allocatable|> {"nodepool": "general"}
System Response: 18 nodes; cpu allocatable 90% RESERVED by requests but only 30% USED; scheduler can't pack more pods despite idle CPU.
<|call:patch_resource|> {"kind": "Deployment", "namespace": "prod", "name": "api-gateway", "patch": {"spec": {"template": {"spec": {"containers": [{"name": "gateway", "resources": {"requests": {"cpu": "300m"}, "limits": {"cpu": "1"}}}]}}}}}
System Response: rolled out; scheduler re-packed pods; CA consolidated 18 -> 7 nodes; CPU headroom (limit 1 = ~3x p99) preserved.
Resolution: Root cause was a CPU request ~9x actual usage (the Guaranteed QoS made it look correct), wrecking bin-packing; right-sized request to 300m with a 1-CPU limit for burst headroom, letting CA drop from 18 to 7 nodes.

Reasoning: The complaint is latency-to-scale, not steady state. Snapshot the HPA status first to rule out a current fault before digging into ramp behavior.
<|call:get_hpa_status|> {"namespace": "prod", "name": "search-api"}
System Response: currentReplicas: 25, desiredReplicas: 25, cpu 68%/70%; conditions: AbleToScale=True, ScalingActive=True, ScalingLimited=False. All green.
Recovery reasoning: Everything is green now — the trap is that the incident already passed, so status tells me nothing about the 9-minute lag. The lag lives in the scale-UP behavior policy. Inspect it.
<|call:get_hpa_behavior|> {"namespace": "prod", "name": "search-api"}
System Response: behavior.scaleUp.stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300; policies: [{type: Percent, value: 50, periodSeconds: 60}]. scaleDown window: 600.
Recovery reasoning: A 300s stabilization window on scale-UP makes the HPA take the MIN recommendation over the last 5 minutes before acting — that's the 9-minute ramp. Someone applied a scale-down-style window to scale-up. Zero the scale-up window and add a fast policy; leave scale-down conservative to avoid thrash.
<|call:patch_resource|> {"kind": "HorizontalPodAutoscaler", "namespace": "prod", "name": "search-api", "patch": {"spec": {"behavior": {"scaleUp": {"stabilizationWindowSeconds": 0, "policies": [{"type": "Percent", "value": 100, "periodSeconds": 30}]}}}}}
System Response: applied; scaleDown window still 600; replay of a CPU breach now scales within 30s instead of 9m.
Resolution: Root cause was a 300s stabilization window mistakenly placed on scaleUp, forcing the HPA to wait out a 5-minute window before ramping; set scaleUp window to 0 with a 100%/30s policy while keeping the 600s scale-down window so we still don't flap.

Reasoning: Saturated but pinned at a round number screams a maxReplicas ceiling. Read the HPA status to confirm it's the cap (ScalingLimited) and not a metrics problem.
<|call:get_hpa_status|> {"namespace": "prod", "name": "payments-worker"}
System Response: currentReplicas: 20, desiredReplicas: 35; conditions: ScalingLimited=True reason=TooManyReplicas "desired replica count is more than the maximum (20)"; cpu 99%/70%.
Recovery reasoning: Confirmed — the HPA WANTS 35 but maxReplicas=20 caps it. Before raising, I must not exhaust the shared Postgres connection pool: 35 workers could blow past max_connections. Check DB connection headroom first.
<|call:query_prometheus|> {"query": "sum(pg_stat_activity_count{datname=\"payments\"}) / max(pg_settings_max_connections)"}
System Response: 0.45 — 135 of 300 connections used at 20 workers (~6.75/worker). 40 workers projects to ~270/300, still under limit.
<|call:patch_resource|> {"kind": "HorizontalPodAutoscaler", "namespace": "prod", "name": "payments-worker", "patch": {"spec": {"maxReplicas": 40}}}
System Response: maxReplicas 20 -> 40; HPA scaled 20 -> 34; queue depth falling; DB at ~230/300 connections.
Resolution: Root cause was the HPA hitting maxReplicas=20 (ScalingLimited/TooManyReplicas); verified the Postgres pool could absorb the extra workers before raising max to 40 — queue draining with the connection-limit invariant preserved.

Reasoning: A reclaim cascade means replacements keep landing on the same vulnerable capacity. First check where session-store pods are right now and their health.
<|call:describe_pod|> {"namespace": "prod", "selector": "app=session-store"}
System Response: 10/10 Running, all Ready, last restart 2m ago. Looks fully recovered.
Recovery reasoning: 10/10 Running is the trap — they're freshly rescheduled onto the SAME risky pool and one more reclaim wave will repeat the outage. Look at the interruption pattern to see what's being taken.
<|call:get_spot_events|> {"region": "us-east-1", "window": "30m"}
System Response: 14 spot interruption notices in the last 30m, ALL instanceType c6i.large in AZ us-east-1a; no interruptions for any other type/AZ.
Recovery reasoning: Every reclaim is one instance type in one AZ — the node group is single-type, single-AZ spot with no diversification, so a single capacity dip wipes the majority. Confirm and diversify, adding a small on-demand base for this stateful service.
<|call:get_nodepool_config|> {"name": "spot-pool"}
System Response: instance_types: [c6i.large], azs: [us-east-1a], capacity: 100% spot, on_demand_base: 0.
<|call:update_nodepool|> {"name": "spot-pool", "instance_types": ["c6i.large", "c6a.large", "c5.large", "m6i.large"], "azs": ["us-east-1a", "us-east-1b", "us-east-1c"], "on_demand_base": 2}
System Response: mixed-instances policy applied; pods spread across 3 AZs and 4 types; 2 replicas pinned to on-demand; next simulated reclaim took only 1 pod.
Resolution: Root cause was a single-type/single-AZ spot pool, so one reclaim wave took the majority and replacements re-landed on the same risk; diversified across 4 instance types and 3 AZs and added a 2-node on-demand base so no single interruption can break quorum for the stateful store.

Reasoning: TARGET 0 on an external metric while the real queue is 50k means the metrics adapter, not the queue, is the problem. Query the external metrics API directly to see what the HPA actually receives.
<|call:query_custom_metrics_api|> {"api": "external.metrics.k8s.io", "metric": "rabbitmq_queue_messages", "namespace": "prod"}
System Response: Error from server (NotFound): the server could not find the metric rabbitmq_queue_messages for namespace prod.
Recovery reasoning: NotFound means prometheus-adapter isn't exposing the series at all, even though Prometheus has it. Inspect the adapter rules to find why the series is filtered out.
<|call:inspect_k8s_resource|> {"kind": "configmap", "namespace": "monitoring", "name": "prometheus-adapter"}
System Response: external rule seriesQuery: 'rabbitmq_queue_messages{exchange="orders"}'. But the notifications queue publishes with exchange="" (default), so the filtered series is empty -> metric not registered.
Recovery reasoning: The seriesQuery label filter exchange="orders" excludes the actual queue series, so the adapter never registers the metric. Loosen the filter to match queues regardless of exchange, then the adapter must be restarted to re-discover series.
<|call:patch_resource|> {"kind": "ConfigMap", "namespace": "monitoring", "name": "prometheus-adapter", "patch": {"rule": "external", "seriesQuery": "rabbitmq_queue_messages{queue!=''}", "metricsQuery": "sum by (queue) (<<.Series>>)"}}
System Response: configmap updated (adapter caches discovery until restart).
<|call:restart_deployment|> {"namespace": "monitoring", "name": "prometheus-adapter"}
System Response: adapter restarted; external.metrics now returns rabbitmq_queue_messages=50120 for prod; HPA TARGET 50120/5000; scaled 2 -> 18.
Resolution: Root cause was a prometheus-adapter seriesQuery whose exchange="orders" filter excluded the notifications queue, so the metric was NotFound and the HPA saw 0; broadened the filter and restarted the adapter to re-register the series, restoring scale-up.

Reasoning: KEDA pinned at the fallback replica count while lag is huge means the trigger isn't reporting. Check the ScaledObject status conditions first.
<|call:check_keda_scaledobject|> {"namespace": "prod", "name": "order-consumer"}
System Response: conditions: Ready=True ("ScaledObject is defined correctly and is ready for scaling"), Active=False; fallback.replicas=1 currently applied.
Recovery reasoning: Ready=True looks healthy — that's the trap; it only means the spec parsed. Active=False is the real signal: the trigger evaluated to no/zero metric, so KEDA used the fallback. Read the KEDA operator log for this trigger.
<|call:check_autoscaler_logs|> {"component": "keda-operator", "filter": "order-consumer"}
System Response: error scaling kafka source: kafka: SASL authentication failed (group lag query); applying fallback (replicas=1) per scaledobject.spec.fallback.
Recovery reasoning: KEDA can't query lag because the Kafka SASL credentials it reads via TriggerAuthentication were rotated, so it silently fell back to 1. Refresh the secret the TriggerAuthentication points at.
<|call:patch_resource|> {"kind": "Secret", "namespace": "prod", "name": "kafka-keda-creds", "patch": {"stringData": {"sasl-password": "<rotated-2026-06-27>"}}}
System Response: secret updated; KEDA re-evaluated trigger within one polling interval; lag=200120 read OK; Active=True; scaled 1 -> 50, lag draining.
Resolution: Root cause was rotated Kafka SASL creds breaking KEDA's lag query, so it fell back to minReplicaCount=1 (while Ready=True masked it); refreshing the credential secret let the trigger read lag again and scale to 50.

Reasoning: Pending pods plus zone-only growth in 1a points at a spread-constraint vs CA-zone mismatch. Read the scheduler's exact reason for the Pending pods first.
<|call:list_pending_pods|> {"namespace": "prod", "selector": "app=web-front"}
System Response: 0/41 nodes available: 18 node(s) didn't match pod topology spread constraints, 23 Insufficient cpu. (1a has spare CPU but adding there would violate maxSkew:1.)
Recovery reasoning: The spread constraint forbids piling more into 1a, but CA only grows 1a, so the pods can never place. Why does CA ignore 1b/1c? Inspect the node group layout and CA balancing config.
<|call:get_nodepool_config|> {"name": "general"}
System Response: 3 single-zone groups general-1a/1b/1c (all min1/max20, identical shape); cluster-autoscaler flags: expander=least-waste, balance-similar-node-groups=FALSE.
Recovery reasoning: With balancing off and a least-waste expander, CA always grows the cheapest single group (1a) instead of spreading, starving 1b/1c and violating the spread constraint. Enable balance-similar-node-groups so CA grows the zones evenly.
<|call:patch_resource|> {"kind": "Deployment", "namespace": "kube-system", "name": "cluster-autoscaler", "patch": {"spec": {"template": {"spec": {"containers": [{"name": "cluster-autoscaler", "args": ["--balance-similar-node-groups=true", "--expander=least-waste"]}]}}}}}
System Response: CA restarted with balancing; next scale-up added nodes to 1b and 1c; maxSkew:1 satisfied; all web-front pods scheduled.
Resolution: Root cause was cluster-autoscaler growing only one zonal node group (balance-similar-node-groups=false + least-waste), starving 1b/1c so the maxSkew:1 spread constraint left pods Pending; enabling node-group balancing spread capacity across zones and unblocked scheduling.

Reasoning: HPA-on-CPU and VPA-on-CPU is a classic conflict — one replicates, the other resizes, both on the same signal. Check the VPA's update mode first; only Auto/Recreate evicts.
<|call:inspect_k8s_resource|> {"kind": "vpa", "namespace": "ml", "name": "ml-inference"}
System Response: VerticalPodAutoscaler 'ml-inference': updatePolicy.updateMode=Off (recommend-only). No eviction from this object.
Recovery reasoning: This VPA is Off, so it can't be evicting — that's the trap; pods ARE being evicted, so a SECOND actor must be resizing them. List every VPA pointing at this Deployment.
<|call:inspect_k8s_resource|> {"kind": "vpa", "namespace": "ml", "selector": "targetRef.name=ml-inference"}
System Response: 2 VPAs: 'ml-inference' (Off, ours) and 'ml-inference-auto' (updateMode=Auto, controlledResources=[cpu,memory]) auto-created by a platform default policy.
Recovery reasoning: The platform's Auto VPA evicts pods to resize CPU requests while the HPA scales replicas on CPU% — they fight over the same resource, causing eviction + oscillation. HPA and VPA must not both own CPU. Set the rogue VPA to Off so the HPA solely owns CPU scaling.
<|call:patch_resource|> {"kind": "VerticalPodAutoscaler", "namespace": "ml", "name": "ml-inference-auto", "patch": {"spec": {"updatePolicy": {"updateMode": "Off"}}}}
System Response: updateMode Auto -> Off; evictions stopped immediately; HPA scales cleanly on CPU; replicas stable at 12 for 15m.
Resolution: Root cause was a platform-default VPA in Auto mode resizing CPU requests while the HPA scaled on CPU% (the first VPA being Off masked it); setting that VPA to Off left the HPA as sole owner of CPU scaling, ending evictions and oscillation.

Reasoning: CA can't remove a node if it can't safely evict a pod on it. Read CA's scale-down log to see exactly which pod/PDB is blocking.
<|call:check_autoscaler_logs|> {"filter": "scale-down"}
System Response: node ip-10-0-3-21: cannot be removed: pod data/zookeeper-1 blocked by PodDisruptionBudget 'zk-pdb' (0 disruptions currently allowed). Repeated for 6 nodes.
Recovery reasoning: 0 disruptions allowed means the PDB is too strict for the replica count. Inspect zk-pdb to see the math.
<|call:check_pdb|> {"namespace": "data", "name": "zk-pdb"}
System Response: spec.minAvailable=3; matched pods=3; currentHealthy=3; disruptionsAllowed=0. With minAvailable==replicas, CA can never drain even one pod.
Recovery reasoning: minAvailable=3 on a 3-replica set permanently forbids eviction. ZooKeeper only needs a quorum majority (2 of 3) to stay consistent, so the correct PDB allows exactly one disruption at a time. Switch to maxUnavailable=1, which keeps 2/3 up — preserving quorum.
<|call:patch_resource|> {"kind": "PodDisruptionBudget", "namespace": "data", "name": "zk-pdb", "patch": {"spec": {"minAvailable": null, "maxUnavailable": 1}}}
System Response: PDB now allows 1 disruption; CA drained and removed 6 nodes one at a time; ZK kept >=2/3 (quorum) throughout; utilization rose to ~55%.
Resolution: Root cause was a PDB minAvailable=3 on a 3-replica StatefulSet permitting zero disruptions, which pinned otherwise-idle nodes; switching to maxUnavailable=1 let CA drain nodes serially while ZooKeeper retained quorum (2/3).

Reasoning: Desired 30 but stuck at 18 — either the HPA capped it or something below the HPA can't create pods. Read the HPA status to see whether the HPA is the limiter.
<|call:get_hpa_status|> {"namespace": "team-bravo", "name": "export-worker"}
System Response: currentReplicas: 18, desiredReplicas: 30; conditions: AbleToScale=True, ScalingActive=True, ScalingLimited=False. The HPA reports no cap.
Recovery reasoning: HPA says ScalingLimited=False and desires 30 — the trap is that it looks healthy, so the bottleneck is BELOW it: the ReplicaSet can't create the extra pods. Check the workload events.
<|call:get_k8s_events|> {"namespace": "team-bravo", "selector": "app=export-worker"}
System Response: Warning FailedCreate (replicaset export-worker-7c): Error creating: pods "export-worker-..." is forbidden: exceeded quota: team-bravo-quota, used: pods=50, limited: pods=50.
Recovery reasoning: The namespace ResourceQuota caps pods at 50 and we're at 50, so the ReplicaSet silently stops at 18 for this app. Before raising it, confirm which dimension is binding so I don't grant CPU/mem the team doesn't have.
<|call:inspect_k8s_resource|> {"kind": "resourcequota", "namespace": "team-bravo", "name": "team-bravo-quota"}
System Response: hard: pods=50 (used 50, BINDING), cpu=80 (used 40), memory=160Gi (used 80Gi). Only the pod count is exhausted; cpu/mem have headroom.
<|call:patch_resource|> {"kind": "ResourceQuota", "namespace": "team-bravo", "name": "team-bravo-quota", "patch": {"spec": {"hard": {"pods": "80"}}}}
System Response: pods quota 50 -> 80; ReplicaSet created the remaining 12 pods; HPA reached 30; cpu/memory still within quota.
Resolution: Root cause was the namespace ResourceQuota pod count (50/50) blocking pod creation while the HPA reported ScalingLimited=False; confirmed only the pod dimension was binding (cpu/mem had room) and raised the pod quota to 80 so the Deployment could reach the HPA's desired 30.

Reasoning: A unit that flaps and then refuses to start has almost certainly tripped StartLimitBurst — systemd stops trying once N restarts happen inside StartLimitIntervalSec. Probe live status first to read the verdict.
<|call:systemctl_status|> {"unit": "api-gateway.service", "host": "prod-edge-3"}
System Response: ● api-gateway.service - API Gateway; Active: failed (Result: start-limit-hit); 'start request repeated too quickly'
Recovery reasoning: Confirmed start-limit-hit, but that is the symptom; the gateway is crashing for a real reason. If I just reset-failed and restart, it re-trips the limit. Read why it crashes first.
<|call:journalctl_query|> {"unit": "api-gateway.service", "lines": 30, "since": "20 min ago"}
System Response: panic: listen tcp :8443: bind: address already in use — exit status 2 (repeated x7)
Recovery reasoning: A stale listener is holding :8443, so every restart crashes and burns start-limit budget. Find the holder before resetting the counter.
<|call:check_port_holder|> {"port": 8443, "host": "prod-edge-3"}
System Response: pid 4127 (api-gateway, orphaned from previous unit generation) still bound to :8443
Recovery reasoning: An orphaned process from a prior generation never died and squats the port. Reap it, reset the start-limit counter, then let systemd start clean.
<|call:reset_failed|> {"unit": "api-gateway.service", "kill_orphan_pid": 4127}
System Response: orphan 4127 reaped; counter reset; api-gateway.service active (running); :8443 bound by new main PID
Resolution: Root cause was an orphaned listener holding :8443 that crash-looped the unit into start-limit-hit; reaping it plus reset-failed restored service without weakening StartLimitBurst.

Reasoning: A service that 'Stopped cleanly' on a fixed cadence under load smells like the cgroup OOM killer, which systemd can log as a clean stop. The journal line alone is misleading — check memory pressure against MemoryMax.
<|call:journalctl_query|> {"unit": "billing-worker.service", "lines": 15, "host": "worker-07"}
System Response: billing-worker.service: Deactivated successfully. Stopped Billing Worker. (repeats every ~6m, no error)
Recovery reasoning: It looks like a graceful stop — but nothing requests a periodic restart, so 'successfully' is the trap. Read the cgroup memory peak vs limit to see if the kernel reaped it.
<|call:read_cgroup|> {"unit": "billing-worker.service", "fields": ["memory.max", "memory.peak", "memory.events"]}
System Response: memory.max=536870912 (512M); memory.peak=536870912; memory.events oom_kill=14 max=22
Recovery reasoning: oom_kill=14 points at the cgroup OOM killer hitting the 512M cap each cycle. Confirm in the ring buffer it is memcg-scoped, not a host-wide OOM, before raising the cap.
<|call:check_dmesg|> {"grep": "oom-kill", "host": "worker-07"}
System Response: oom-kill: constraint=CONSTRAINT_MEMCG oom_memcg=/system.slice/billing-worker.service tasks killed
Recovery reasoning: Memcg-scoped OOM confirmed, not host-wide. The working set outgrew 512M; raise MemoryMax via a drop-in (not the vendor unit) and add MemoryHigh for soft back-pressure before the hard kill.
<|call:write_drop_in|> {"unit": "billing-worker.service", "file": "override.conf", "content": "[Service]\nMemoryHigh=896M\nMemoryMax=1G"}
System Response: drop-in written; daemon-reload + restart applied; memory.peak plateaus at 870M; no oom_kill in 30m
Resolution: Root cause was the cgroup OOM killer at MemoryMax=512M masquerading as a clean stop; a drop-in raising MemoryMax to 1G with a MemoryHigh soft cap stopped the kills while keeping per-service accounting intact.

Reasoning: systemd breaks ordering cycles by deleting a job from the loop — often the one you actually wanted. Run verify to see which edge it severed.
<|call:systemd_analyze|> {"verb": "verify", "unit": "app-db.service"}
System Response: app-db.service: Found ordering cycle on app-db.service/start; job app-db.service/start deleted to break cycle
Recovery reasoning: The trap: systemd 'fixed' the cycle by dropping app-db's own start job, so it silently never runs. I need the real edges to know which After= to cut.
<|call:list_dependencies|> {"unit": "mount-data.service", "reverse": false}
System Response: mount-data.service After=app-db.service; app-db.service After=mount-data.service Requires=mount-data.service
Recovery reasoning: Both units order After= each other — app-db legitimately needs the mount, but mount-data got a bogus After=app-db.service. Clear that stray edge, keeping app-db's Requires/After on the mount.
<|call:write_drop_in|> {"unit": "mount-data.service", "file": "override.conf", "content": "[Unit]\nAfter=\n"}
System Response: override resets After=, removing After=app-db.service; daemon-reload; analyze verify: no ordering cycle
Recovery reasoning: Cycle gone; now ensure the previously-deleted start job actually runs on this boot.
<|call:start_unit|> {"unit": "app-db.service"}
System Response: mount-data.service active (mounted) -> app-db.service active (running)
Resolution: Root cause was a reciprocal After= edge that made systemd delete app-db's start job to break the cycle; clearing the stray After=app-db on mount-data restored one-directional ordering and both units start.

Reasoning: A unit that 'enables' fine yet never executes is the classic masked-unit trap — enable can return 0 while the unit is masked, because enable does not override a mask. Reproduce the deploy's enable to see what it really did.
<|call:enable_unit|> {"unit": "cleanup-tmp.service"}
System Response: Created symlink /etc/systemd/system/timers.target.wants/cleanup-tmp.timer; exit 0 (reported success)
Recovery reasoning: enable returned 0 — that is the trap; it does not prove the service can start. Check the load/file state.
<|call:get_property|> {"unit": "cleanup-tmp.service", "property": "LoadState"}
System Response: LoadState=masked; UnitFileState=masked
Recovery reasoning: Masked, not merely disabled — so every timer-triggered start is silently swallowed. Confirm the mask target before unmasking so I do not clobber a deliberate reason.
<|call:systemctl_cat|> {"unit": "cleanup-tmp.service"}
System Response: # /etc/systemd/system/cleanup-tmp.service -> /dev/null
Recovery reasoning: A leftover mask (symlink to /dev/null) from a past incident; the deploy's enable could never override it. Unmask, then trigger once to confirm it reclaims disk.
<|call:unmask_unit|> {"unit": "cleanup-tmp.service", "then_start": true}
System Response: unmasked; symlink now -> /etc/systemd/system/cleanup-tmp.service; started; ExecMainStatus=0; /tmp freed 41G, disk 57%
Resolution: Root cause was a leftover mask (symlink to /dev/null) that made every timer-triggered start a silent no-op while enable still returned 0; unmasking restored the real unit and the cleanup reclaimed 41G.

Reasoning: A log gap during a spike with the service still serving 200s is almost always journald rate-limiting, not a crash — and a restart would be a needless outage. Confirm the unit is actually up before touching anything.
<|call:systemctl_status|> {"unit": "ingest-api.service"}
System Response: ● ingest-api.service - Active: active (running) since 3h ago; Main PID 2231; Tasks 88; CPU healthy
Recovery reasoning: Service is healthy and busy — the 'quiet journal' is the trap, not a crash. Look for journald's own suppression notices around the gap.
<|call:journalctl_query|> {"unit": "systemd-journald", "grep": "Suppressed", "since": "10 min ago"}
System Response: Suppressed 18342 messages from ingest-api.service (ratelimit: 10000 burst / 30s)
Recovery reasoning: journald dropped 18k lines because the unit blew past RateLimitBurst during the spike. Raise the per-unit log rate limit via a drop-in rather than disabling rate-limiting globally, so other units stay protected.
<|call:write_drop_in|> {"unit": "ingest-api.service", "file": "override.conf", "content": "[Service]\nLogRateLimitIntervalSec=30s\nLogRateLimitBurst=200000"}
System Response: drop-in applied; daemon-reload + restart; journal now continuous under load test; zero Suppressed lines
Resolution: Root cause was journald rate-limiting (10k/30s) silently dropping logs during the spike and mimicking a crash; a per-unit LogRateLimitBurst override restored continuous logging without weakening global journald limits and avoided a needless restart.

Reasoning: A oneshot whose job completes but then reads inactive, causing dependents to re-trigger it, is the missing-RemainAfterExit pattern. Confirm the oneshot actually succeeded first.
<|call:get_property|> {"unit": "load-firewall.service", "property": "ExecMainStatus"}
System Response: ExecMainStatus=0 (SUCCESS); ActiveState=inactive; SubState=dead
Recovery reasoning: The trap: it succeeded (status 0) yet ActiveState=inactive, so systemd treats the requirement as unmet and re-runs it for the next dependent. Check the unit for RemainAfterExit before editing.
<|call:systemctl_cat|> {"unit": "load-firewall.service"}
System Response: [Service] Type=oneshot ExecStart=/usr/sbin/nft -f /etc/nftables.conf (no RemainAfterExit set)
Recovery reasoning: RemainAfterExit is absent, so every dependent's Requires= forces a re-apply. Add RemainAfterExit=yes via a drop-in so the unit latches active after loading the rules once. Write it, then reload to apply.
<|call:write_drop_in|> {"unit": "load-firewall.service", "file": "override.conf", "content": "[Service]\nRemainAfterExit=yes"}
System Response: drop-in written to override.conf (not yet active until daemon-reload)
Recovery reasoning: Reload so systemd re-reads the unit; with RemainAfterExit the unit should now stay active (exited) and dependents should see the requirement satisfied without a re-run.
<|call:daemon_reload|> {"unit": "load-firewall.service", "restart": false}
System Response: reloaded; unit now active (exited); dependents see requirement satisfied; nft applied exactly once
Resolution: Root cause was a Type=oneshot unit missing RemainAfterExit, so it dropped to inactive after success and dependents re-ran it; adding RemainAfterExit=yes latches it active and prevents the duplicate nftables apply.

Reasoning: With socket activation, the .socket owning the fd while the .service ALSO binds the same port causes EADDRINUSE on activation — but the socket still shows LISTEN, so it looks healthy. Probe the socket unit first.
<|call:socket_status|> {"unit": "metrics-collector.socket", "port": 9100}
System Response: metrics-collector.socket: active (listening); LISTEN 0.0.0.0:9100 (looks healthy)
Recovery reasoning: The socket is listening — that is the trap; the failure is on the service side when it tries to bind the same port itself. Check the service's activation logs.
<|call:journalctl_query|> {"unit": "metrics-collector.service", "lines": 12}
System Response: activated by socket; bind 0.0.0.0:9100: address already in use; exit 1; connection handed back unserved
Recovery reasoning: The service opens its own listener instead of inheriting the socket-activated fd via sd_listen_fds. Confirm the ExecStart flags before switching it to fd inheritance.
<|call:systemctl_cat|> {"unit": "metrics-collector.service"}
System Response: ExecStart=/usr/bin/metrics-collector --listen 0.0.0.0:9100 (ignores $LISTEN_FDS)
Recovery reasoning: It re-binds :9100 instead of using the inherited fd. Drop-in to switch to --systemd-socket fd inheritance and drop --listen so only the .socket owns the port.
<|call:write_drop_in|> {"unit": "metrics-collector.service", "file": "override.conf", "content": "[Service]\nExecStart=\nExecStart=/usr/bin/metrics-collector --systemd-socket"}
System Response: drop-in applied; daemon-reload; service now inherits fd 3; no bind errors; scrapes 200 steady for 15m
Resolution: Root cause was the service re-binding :9100 instead of inheriting the socket-activated fd, racing the .socket and throwing EADDRINUSE while the socket still showed LISTEN; switching ExecStart to fd inheritance let the socket own the port and stabilized scrapes.

Reasoning: Type=notify with ~WatchdogSec restarts while the app logs look busy is the classic 'app stopped pinging the watchdog' case — sd_notify WATCHDOG=1 isn't being sent even though work continues. Read the app's own logs first; they'll look healthy, which is the trap.
<|call:journalctl_query|> {"unit": "stream-processor.service", "lines": 8}
System Response: processed batch 8841 ok; processed batch 8842 ok; processed batch 8843 ok; processed batch 8844 ok (steady, no errors)
Recovery reasoning: App logs are all green — but 'busy' is not the same as 'pinging the watchdog'. Check systemd's watchdog state and last keepalive.
<|call:check_watchdog|> {"unit": "stream-processor.service"}
System Response: WatchdogUSec=30000000 (30s); NotifyAccess=main; last WATCHDOG=1 received 31s ago; last action SIGABRT (x40)
Recovery reasoning: systemd hasn't gotten WATCHDOG=1 in 31s — a long batch blocks the worker loop past the 30s window. Rather than disable the watchdog (loses crash detection), raise WatchdogSec above worst-case batch latency.
<|call:write_drop_in|> {"unit": "stream-processor.service", "file": "override.conf", "content": "[Service]\nWatchdogSec=120"}
System Response: drop-in applied; daemon-reload + restart; WATCHDOG=1 now received every ~25s within the 120s window; no SIGABRT in 20m
Resolution: Root cause was long batches starving the sd_notify WATCHDOG=1 keepalive under a 30s WatchdogSec, so systemd SIGABRT-restarted a healthy worker; raising WatchdogSec to 120s past worst-case batch latency stopped the kills while keeping watchdog crash-detection.

Reasoning: Type=forking that times out as 'failed' while the daemon is actually running points to a PIDFile mismatch — systemd waits for a PID file path the daemon never writes. Confirm the process really is up first.
<|call:check_port_holder|> {"port": 80, "host": "lb-11"}
System Response: pid 5567 nginx: master process (running, 4 workers, serving requests) — looks healthy
Recovery reasoning: nginx is genuinely serving — so 'failed' is the trap; systemd just can't track it. Compare the PIDFile systemd expects against where nginx writes it.
<|call:get_property|> {"unit": "nginx-edge.service", "property": "PIDFile"}
System Response: PIDFile=/run/nginx-edge.pid; but nginx.conf 'pid' directive writes /run/nginx.pid
Recovery reasoning: Mismatch confirmed — systemd watches /run/nginx-edge.pid which never appears, times out, then SIGKILLs on the next cycle. Align the unit's PIDFile to the path nginx actually writes, then reload and restart cleanly.
<|call:write_drop_in|> {"unit": "nginx-edge.service", "file": "override.conf", "content": "[Service]\nPIDFile=/run/nginx.pid"}
System Response: drop-in applied; daemon-reload; restart -> active (running) in 0.6s; MainPID tracks the master; status green
Resolution: Root cause was a PIDFile path mismatch (unit expected /run/nginx-edge.pid, nginx wrote /run/nginx.pid) causing forking-type start to time out as failed while nginx ran; aligning PIDFile let systemd track the master PID and report accurately.

Reasoning: SIGTERM at exactly 1m30s is the default TimeoutStartSec=90s for a service whose start never signals ready — a 4-minute migration simply outruns the start timeout. Confirm which timeout fired.
<|call:journalctl_query|> {"unit": "schema-migrate.service", "lines": 12}
System Response: Flyway migrating V37->V38...; schema-migrate.service: start operation timed out. Terminating.; Failed with result 'timeout'
Recovery reasoning: Timeout at start, not a migration error — but blindly bumping the timeout on a half-applied schema is risky. Check the type and whether migrations are transactional before extending.
<|call:get_property|> {"unit": "schema-migrate.service", "property": "TimeoutStartUSec"}
System Response: Type=oneshot; TimeoutStartUSec=1min 30s; RemainAfterExit=yes
Recovery reasoning: Oneshot on the default 90s start timeout. Flyway wraps each migration in a transaction, so extending is safe — raise TimeoutStartSec well past the 4-minute job with margin, then re-run.
<|call:write_drop_in|> {"unit": "schema-migrate.service", "file": "override.conf", "content": "[Service]\nTimeoutStartSec=900"}
System Response: drop-in applied; daemon-reload; re-run: V37->V38->V39 applied in 3m51s; ExecMainStatus=0; schema consistent
Resolution: Root cause was the default 90s TimeoutStartSec killing a 4-minute migration mid-run; raising it to 900s let Flyway's transactional migration complete, and RemainAfterExit kept the success latched without partial-schema risk.

Reasoning: 'fork: Resource temporarily unavailable' with free RAM and a high ulimit -u usually means the systemd per-unit TasksMax (cgroup pids.max), which is independent of the login ulimit. Probe the live task count vs the cgroup limit.
<|call:read_cgroup|> {"unit": "ci-builder.service", "fields": ["pids.current", "pids.max"]}
System Response: pids.current=512; pids.max=512
Recovery reasoning: pids.max=512 is the wall, not the 64000 ulimit — the trap is that ulimit -u looks generous while the cgroup caps it. Confirm where 512 comes from before raising it.
<|call:get_property|> {"unit": "ci-builder.service", "property": "TasksMax"}
System Response: TasksMax=512 (explicit on the unit, overriding system.conf DefaultTasksMax)
Recovery reasoning: TasksMax is pinned at 512 on the unit. Raise it via drop-in to a value above peak parallel jobs but still bounded to prevent a fork-bomb, then reload and verify the cgroup ceiling moved.
<|call:write_drop_in|> {"unit": "ci-builder.service", "file": "override.conf", "content": "[Service]\nTasksMax=8192"}
System Response: drop-in applied; daemon-reload + restart; pids.max=8192; a 900-job parallel build completes with no fork failures
Resolution: Root cause was the unit's TasksMax=512 (cgroup pids.max) capping forks regardless of the 64000 login ulimit; raising TasksMax to a bounded 8192 cleared the fork failures while still preventing runaway process growth.

Reasoning: 'too many open files' at exactly ~1024 while limits.conf says 1048576 is the giveaway that pam_limits (limits.conf) does not apply to systemd-started services — they use LimitNOFILE, whose soft default is low. Confirm the effective soft limit on the running process.
<|call:check_rlimit|> {"unit": "socket-relay.service", "resource": "NOFILE"}
System Response: LimitNOFILE hard=524288; LimitNOFILE soft=1024 (effective for the process)
Recovery reasoning: Hard=524288 but soft=1024, and limits.conf is irrelevant here (the trap). The app never raises its own soft limit, so it caps at 1024. Confirm the in-kernel fd count is at that ceiling.
<|call:read_cgroup|> {"unit": "socket-relay.service", "fields": ["fd.current", "fd.soft_limit"]}
System Response: fd.current=1021; fd.soft_limit=1024 (at the ceiling, EMFILE on accept)
Recovery reasoning: Sitting at the 1024 soft ceiling. Set LimitNOFILE=soft:hard explicitly on the unit so the process starts with a high soft limit, then reload, restart, and confirm headroom.
<|call:write_drop_in|> {"unit": "socket-relay.service", "file": "override.conf", "content": "[Service]\nLimitNOFILE=262144:524288"}
System Response: drop-in applied; daemon-reload + restart; soft limit now 262144; fd.current climbs to 8400 with no EMFILE; connections stable
Resolution: Root cause was the systemd default soft LimitNOFILE of 1024 (limits.conf does not govern systemd units) throwing EMFILE on accept; setting LimitNOFILE=262144:524288 on the unit gave the relay the fd headroom it needed.

Reasoning: A timer that is 'active' yet hasn't fired in days usually means a wrong OnCalendar/next-elapse or a missed window with no Persistent catch-up — is-active=active is a misleading green. Read the actual next/last trigger.
<|call:list_timers|> {"unit": "nightly-backup.timer"}
System Response: NEXT=Sat 2026-07-04 02:30; LAST=n/a; PASSED=n/a; UNIT=nightly-backup.timer ACTIVATES nightly-backup.service
Recovery reasoning: NEXT is a week out and LAST=n/a — the trap is that 'active' only means the timer loaded, not that it ever triggered. A NEXT that far implies a bad OnCalendar. Inspect the timer definition.
<|call:systemctl_cat|> {"unit": "nightly-backup.timer"}
System Response: [Timer] OnCalendar=Sat *-*-* 02:30:00 (intended daily, but pinned to Saturday); Persistent not set
Recovery reasoning: OnCalendar was accidentally pinned to Saturday so it fired weekly, and with no Persistent it can't catch up a missed run. Fix the calendar to daily and add Persistent=true so a missed window runs at next boot.
<|call:write_drop_in|> {"unit": "nightly-backup.timer", "file": "override.conf", "content": "[Timer]\nOnCalendar=\nOnCalendar=*-*-* 02:30:00\nPersistent=true"}
System Response: drop-in applied; daemon-reload; NEXT=tomorrow 02:30; Persistent armed; manual trigger runs a catch-up snapshot now
Recovery reasoning: Calendar corrected and a catch-up snapshot taken; confirm the service completed so the 3-day gap is closed.
<|call:get_property|> {"unit": "nightly-backup.service", "property": "ExecMainStatus"}
System Response: ExecMainStatus=0 (SUCCESS); fresh snapshot timestamp = now; backup chain consistent
Resolution: Root cause was an OnCalendar pinned to Saturday (firing weekly) with no Persistent flag to catch missed runs, while is-active reported the timer 'active'; correcting OnCalendar to daily plus Persistent=true restored nightly backups and a manual run closed the gap.

Reasoning: A service that starts clean yet reports DATABASE_URL empty points to an EnvironmentFile that failed to load silently — a leading '-' on EnvironmentFile makes a missing file non-fatal, so the unit starts with no env. Confirm the env really isn't in the process.
<|call:get_property|> {"unit": "payments-api.service", "property": "Environment"}
System Response: Environment=[unset]; Active: active (running); ExecMainStatus=0 (started clean)
Recovery reasoning: Started clean but Environment is empty — the trap is the green start masking a swallowed env load. Check the EnvironmentFile directive and whether the file survived the rebuild.
<|call:systemctl_cat|> {"unit": "payments-api.service"}
System Response: [Service] EnvironmentFile=-/etc/payments-api/env (leading '-' => optional); ExecStart=/usr/bin/payments-api
Recovery reasoning: The '-' prefix made the missing /etc/payments-api/env non-fatal, so the host rebuild could have dropped it and the service started with no DATABASE_URL. Verify the file is actually absent before recreating it.
<|call:check_file|> {"path": "/etc/payments-api/env", "host": "pay-2"}
System Response: /etc/payments-api/env: No such file or directory (not restored by the host rebuild)
Recovery reasoning: The rebuild never restored the env file. Restore it from config management, reload, restart; keep the '-' optional prefix but add an ExecStartPre assert so a future missing file fails loudly instead of silently.
<|call:restore_config_file|> {"path": "/etc/payments-api/env", "source": "config-mgmt", "then_restart": "payments-api.service"}
System Response: file restored (DATABASE_URL set); daemon-reload + restart; Environment now populated; requests 200; ExecStartPre existence assert added
Resolution: Root cause was the host rebuild dropping /etc/payments-api/env while the EnvironmentFile '-' prefix let the service start without it (DATABASE_URL empty); restoring the file fixed requests, and an ExecStartPre existence assert now makes a missing env fail fast instead of silently.

Reasoning: Every plan now errors on the state lock, so before assuming the holder is dead I reproduce the failure to capture the exact lock ID and holder from the live backend.
<|call:run_terraform_plan|> {"workspace": "prod-vpc"}
System Response: Error: Error acquiring the state lock. Lock Info: ID=7f3a1c, Who=ci-runner@build-9921, Created=02:04Z, Operation=OperationTypeApply
Recovery reasoning: I have the lock ID, but force-unlocking a live apply would corrupt state. Read the lock metadata to inspect the holder before deciding.
<|call:inspect_state_lock|> {"backend": "s3", "lock_table": "tf-locks-prod", "path": "prod-vpc/terraform.tfstate"}
System Response: Lock present: ID=7f3a1c, holder build-9921, age 41m, no heartbeat field (legacy lock row) — liveness cannot be inferred from the row alone
Recovery reasoning: The row can't prove the holder is dead, so confirm the CI build's fate before unlocking, avoiding a race with a real apply.
<|call:check_ci_pipeline_logs|> {"build": "build-9921", "fields": "status,exit_code,ended_at"}
System Response: build-9921 status=KILLED exit_code=137 ended_at=02:21Z (OOMKilled) — no terraform process alive
Recovery reasoning: The holder was OOMKilled at 02:21 with no apply in flight, so the lock is orphaned. Force-unlock by the exact lock ID, never blanket-clearing the table, to preserve other workspaces' locks.
<|call:force_unlock_state|> {"lock_id": "7f3a1c", "path": "prod-vpc/terraform.tfstate", "yes": true}
System Response: Terraform state lock released; the subsequent plan acquired and released the lock cleanly
Resolution: Root cause was an orphaned DynamoDB lock left by an OOMKilled CI apply; a targeted force-unlock by lock ID cleared it without disturbing any other workspace's lock.

Reasoning: Security sees an open SSH rule but the pipeline plan reports clean, so either it ran without refresh or the rule is unmanaged. Reproduce the pipeline plan exactly, including its refresh setting.
<|call:run_terraform_plan|> {"workspace": "prod", "target": "aws_security_group.web", "refresh": false}
System Response: No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration. Plan: 0 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy.
Recovery reasoning: The green result is misleading — the pipeline runs with refresh=false and trusts stale state. Force a refresh-only run to compare config against the live security group.
<|call:terraform_refresh|> {"workspace": "prod", "target": "aws_security_group.web"}
System Response: Refreshing aws_security_group.web — ingress 0.0.0.0/0:22 present in cloud but absent from state and config
Recovery reasoning: A 0.0.0.0/0:22 rule exists out-of-band. Confirm it directly on the cloud resource before letting a plan reconcile it.
<|call:describe_cloud_resource|> {"service": "ec2", "id": "sg-0a1b2c3d", "field": "IpPermissions"}
System Response: IpPermissions includes FromPort 22 ToPort 22 IpRanges 0.0.0.0/0 — manually added, unmanaged by Terraform
Recovery reasoning: Drift is real and unmanaged. Re-run plan WITH refresh so Terraform proposes removing the rogue rule and restores the closed-22 baseline.
<|call:run_terraform_plan|> {"workspace": "prod", "refresh": true}
System Response: Plan: 0 to add, 0 to change, 1 to destroy (unmanaged ingress 0.0.0.0/0:22); applying re-closes port 22 to the world
Resolution: Root cause was the pipeline running plan with refresh=false, masking an out-of-band 0.0.0.0/0:22 ingress rule; re-enabling refresh surfaced the drift so the apply re-closes SSH to the internet.

Reasoning: The import fails on a non-existent address, which usually means the address I gave doesn't match where the resource is declared. Try the import as written to capture the precise error.
<|call:terraform_import|> {"address": "aws_db_instance.orders", "id": "prod-orders-db"}
System Response: Error: resource address aws_db_instance.orders does not exist in configuration — cannot import to non-existent resource address
Recovery reasoning: The resource is probably declared inside a module, so the bare address is wrong. Read the config to find the real declared path.
<|call:read_tf_config|> {"path": "modules/database/main.tf", "match": "aws_db_instance.orders"}
System Response: aws_db_instance.orders is declared inside module database (root call: module.database) — the bare address is invalid
Recovery reasoning: The module-qualified address is module.database.aws_db_instance.orders. Confirm the canonical import id from the cloud before retrying, since the provider expects the DB identifier.
<|call:describe_cloud_resource|> {"service": "rds", "id": "prod-orders-db", "field": "DBInstanceIdentifier"}
System Response: DBInstanceIdentifier prod-orders-db, status available — canonical import id confirmed
Recovery reasoning: Re-run the import with the module-qualified address and the canonical identifier.
<|call:terraform_import|> {"address": "module.database.aws_db_instance.orders", "id": "prod-orders-db"}
System Response: Import successful; state tracks 1 resource; terraform plan shows 0 to change
Resolution: Root cause was an unqualified import address for a resource living in module.database; using the module-qualified address with the canonical DBInstanceIdentifier landed the import with no spurious diff.

Reasoning: init can't satisfy ~> 5.40 against a lock pinned at 5.31.0, so the constraint and the lockfile disagree. Reproduce init to capture the exact conflict before touching anything.
<|call:run_terraform_init|> {"upgrade": false}
System Response: Error: Failed to query available provider packages — no available releases match constraint ~> 5.40; lock records hashicorp/aws 5.31.0
Recovery reasoning: A teammate bumped the constraint but never refreshed the lock. Read the lockfile to confirm the recorded version and platform hashes before re-resolving.
<|call:check_provider_lockfile|> {"path": ".terraform.lock.hcl", "provider": "hashicorp/aws"}
System Response: hashicorp/aws version 5.31.0, constraints ~> 5.31, hashes for linux_amd64 only
Recovery reasoning: The lock is stale at 5.31.0 with linux-only hashes while config now wants ~> 5.40. Run init with upgrade across both CI and dev platforms to re-resolve and rewrite the lock.
<|call:run_terraform_init|> {"upgrade": true, "platforms": ["linux_amd64", "darwin_arm64"]}
System Response: Installed hashicorp/aws 5.40.0; lockfile rewritten with linux_amd64 and darwin_arm64 hashes; init successful
Resolution: Root cause was a versions.tf bump to ~> 5.40 without refreshing the stale 5.31.0 lockfile; init -upgrade re-resolved to 5.40.0 and recorded multi-platform hashes, unblocking CI and the dev macs.

Reasoning: A full destroy/recreate after a count-to-for_each refactor is classic address churn — worker[0] and worker[a] are the same box but Terraform can't see it. Reproduce the plan to capture exactly which live instances are at risk.
<|call:run_terraform_plan|> {"workspace": "prod", "target": "aws_instance.worker"}
System Response: Plan: 3 to add, 3 to destroy — destroys worker[0]=i-0aa1, worker[1]=i-0bb2, worker[2]=i-0cc3 (serving traffic); adds worker[a], worker[b], worker[c]
Recovery reasoning: This is pure address churn, not real change, so the fix is state mv, never apply. Read the for_each map to learn the correct key-to-instance mapping; a naive alphabetical guess could rebind the wrong EC2.
<|call:read_tf_config|> {"path": "workers.tf", "match": "for_each"}
System Response: for_each map keys by role tag: b -> i-0aa1, a -> i-0bb2, c -> i-0cc3 (NOT alphabetical to the old index order)
Recovery reasoning: So i-0aa1 belongs to key b, i-0bb2 to a, i-0cc3 to c. Verify the live state addresses, then move by identity.
<|call:read_terraform_state|> {"filter": "aws_instance.worker", "fields": "address,id"}
System Response: worker[0]=i-0aa1, worker[1]=i-0bb2, worker[2]=i-0cc3 — confirmed
Recovery reasoning: Apply the three identity-correct moves so each instance keeps its key, then re-plan must be clean.
<|call:terraform_state_mv|> {"resource": "aws_instance.worker", "moves": [{"index": 0, "key": "b"}, {"index": 1, "key": "a"}, {"index": 2, "key": "c"}]}
System Response: 3 moves applied (0->b, 1->a, 2->c); terraform plan now reports 0 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy
Resolution: Root cause was index-to-key address churn from the count-to-for_each migration; identity-correct state mv (mapping by role tag, not the misleading alphabetical order) preserved all three live workers with zero recreation.

Reasoning: A tainted cache cluster forces destroy/recreate on next apply, but the operator says it's healthy, so the taint is likely a stale artifact from the half-failed apply. Reproduce the plan to confirm it really wants to replace the cluster.
<|call:run_terraform_plan|> {"workspace": "prod", "target": "aws_elasticache_cluster.sessions"}
System Response: Plan: 1 to add, 1 to destroy — aws_elasticache_cluster.sessions is tainted, forcing replacement of sessions-001 (active user sessions would flush)
Recovery reasoning: Replacement is driven by a taint, not a config diff. Confirm the taint's origin in state before clearing it.
<|call:read_terraform_state|> {"filter": "aws_elasticache_cluster.sessions", "fields": "address,status,id"}
System Response: aws_elasticache_cluster.sessions id=sessions-001 status=tainted (marked by failed apply 02:11Z)
Recovery reasoning: Before untainting I must verify the cluster is genuinely healthy in AWS — untainting a truly broken node would hide a real fault. Check the cloud status.
<|call:describe_cloud_resource|> {"service": "elasticache", "id": "sessions-001", "field": "CacheClusterStatus"}
System Response: CacheClusterStatus available; 1 node healthy; uptime 14d — cluster is serving
Recovery reasoning: The cluster is available, so the taint is purely a state artifact. Untaint just this resource so plan stops forcing a destroy, preserving live sessions.
<|call:terraform_untaint|> {"address": "aws_elasticache_cluster.sessions"}
System Response: Resource aws_elasticache_cluster.sessions untainted; terraform plan now shows 0 to change, 0 to destroy
Resolution: Root cause was a stale taint left by a half-failed apply on a cluster that was actually healthy; verifying availability via the cloud API then untaint cleared the forced recreate without flushing sessions.

Reasoning: The apply went green, so this isn't a failure — a value was printed without the sensitive flag. Read the CI build log to see exactly what leaked and from where.
<|call:check_ci_pipeline_logs|> {"build": "4471", "match": "password|Outputs"}
System Response: Apply complete! Outputs: db_master_password = Pr0d-Analytics-9921 — printed in cleartext at the end of a successful apply
Recovery reasoning: The green apply still leaked because an output exposed the password. Inspect the output definition to confirm the missing sensitive flag.
<|call:read_terraform_output|> {"name": "db_master_password", "show": "definition"}
System Response: output db_master_password value = aws_db_instance.analytics.password; sensitive flag NOT set, so Terraform echoes it to stdout
Recovery reasoning: Mark the output sensitive so future runs redact it; the already-public value must also be rotated. Patch the output first.
<|call:edit_tf_config|> {"path": "outputs.tf", "patch": {"output.db_master_password.sensitive": true}}
System Response: outputs.tf updated; re-running plan shows db_master_password = (sensitive value); CI log now redacts it
Recovery reasoning: Future runs are redacted, but the exposed credential is burned. Rotate the RDS master password to invalidate the leaked value.
<|call:describe_cloud_resource|> {"service": "rds", "id": "analytics", "action": "rotate_master_password"}
System Response: master password rotated; old value invalidated; apps reading from the secrets-manager reference are unaffected
Resolution: Root cause was an output missing sensitive = true, leaking the RDS password to a public CI log despite a green apply; adding the flag redacts future runs and rotating the credential invalidated the exposed value.

Reasoning: One apply 'succeeded' while the other's read is stale on an S3 backend — a likely lost-update race. Read the current state to see whether the NAT gateway is tracked at all.
<|call:read_terraform_state|> {"backend": "s3", "key": "net/terraform.tfstate", "fields": "serial,checksum,resources"}
System Response: serial 412, checksum valid, state reads clean with no corruption; aws_nat_gateway.main absent — looks like it was simply never created
Recovery reasoning: A clean, uncorrupted state is the trap — it can still be a stale version that overwrote a newer write if locking is off. Inspect the backend for a lock table.
<|call:read_backend_config|> {"path": "backend.tf"}
System Response: backend s3 bucket tf-state-net key net/terraform.tfstate — no dynamodb_table set; state locking is DISABLED
Recovery reasoning: With no lock table the two applies raced and a stale write became LATEST, dropping the NAT gateway from state. Confirm the gateway actually exists in AWS before re-importing.
<|call:describe_cloud_resource|> {"service": "ec2", "id": "natgw-01", "field": "State"}
System Response: State available — the NAT gateway exists in AWS, only missing from the latest state version
Recovery reasoning: The gateway is real; re-import it into the current state and enable a DynamoDB lock so a lost-update race can't recur.
<|call:terraform_import|> {"address": "aws_nat_gateway.main", "id": "natgw-01", "enable_lock_table": "tf-locks-net"}
System Response: Import successful; aws_nat_gateway.main back in state; plan shows 0 changes; DynamoDB locking now enforced
Resolution: Root cause was an S3 backend with locking disabled, allowing a lost-update race where a stale apply overwrote the NAT gateway out of state; re-importing the live gateway and enabling DynamoDB locking reconciled state and closed the race.

Reasoning: validate reports a duplicate aws_iam_role.ci_deployer after the merge, so the same role is declared twice. Reproduce validate to capture both declaration sites.
<|call:terraform_validate|> {"path": "modules/iam"}
System Response: Error: Duplicate resource — aws_iam_role.ci_deployer declared at roles.tf:14 and again at ci.tf:8
Recovery reasoning: Both branches added the same role name. Read both blocks to see whether the policies differ, so I merge rather than drop permissions.
<|call:read_tf_config|> {"path": "modules/iam", "match": "aws_iam_role.ci_deployer"}
System Response: roles.tf attaches deploy-s3 + deploy-ecs; ci.tf attaches deploy-lambda + deploy-ecs — same role name, different policy attachments
Recovery reasoning: It's a true duplicate, not two distinct roles. Check the dependency graph so I keep one role block while preserving both attachments.
<|call:terraform_graph|> {"focus": "aws_iam_role.ci_deployer", "show": "dependents"}
System Response: dependents: aws_iam_role_policy_attachment.s3 (roles.tf) and aws_iam_role_policy_attachment.lambda (ci.tf) — both target the one role address
Recovery reasoning: Keep the single role block in roles.tf and remove only the duplicate declaration in ci.tf, leaving its lambda attachment intact, then re-validate.
<|call:edit_tf_config|> {"path": "modules/iam/ci.tf", "remove_block": "aws_iam_role.ci_deployer", "keep": "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment.lambda"}
System Response: duplicate role removed; aws_iam_role.ci_deployer declared once; both s3 and lambda attachments resolve; terraform validate Success; plan shows 0 to destroy
Resolution: Root cause was both merged branches declaring the same aws_iam_role.ci_deployer; consolidating to one role block while keeping both policy attachments fixed validate and preserved every team's permissions.

Reasoning: An apply reported complete but changed the wrong environment — a classic workspace mismatch. Read the apply log to see which workspace it actually ran against and what it changed.
<|call:check_ci_pipeline_logs|> {"build": "local-apply-latest", "match": "Apply complete|Workspace|destroyed"}
System Response: Apply complete! Resources: 0 added, 0 changed, 1 destroyed. Workspace: prod (engineer believed they were in staging)
Recovery reasoning: The green apply ran in prod, not staging, and destroyed one resource. Confirm the currently selected workspace before remediating.
<|call:list_workspaces|> {"show_current": true}
System Response: default, staging, * prod — current workspace is prod; the staging ALB was never touched
Recovery reasoning: The session is pinned to prod, where a listener rule was wrongly destroyed. Explicitly select prod and check its committed config versus state.
<|call:select_workspace|> {"name": "prod"}
System Response: Workspace prod selected (confirmed); prod config in repo defines 12 listener rules, state currently has 11
Recovery reasoning: Re-apply prod's committed config, targeting the dropped rule, to restore the count to 12; the intended staging change must be redone under the staging workspace.
<|call:run_terraform_apply|> {"workspace": "prod", "target": "aws_lb_listener_rule.block_legacy", "auto_approve": false}
System Response: Plan: 1 to add (block_legacy priority 50); applied; prod listener rule count back to 12
Resolution: Root cause was operating in the prod workspace while intending staging, so a green apply destroyed a prod listener rule; selecting prod and re-applying its committed config restored all 12 rules, and the staging edit must be re-run under staging.

Reasoning: init can't find a 4.x for the vpc module though latest is 5.21.0, and our code didn't change, so the registry we resolve against likely no longer serves 4.x. Reproduce init to capture the exact registry and available versions.
<|call:run_terraform_init|> {"upgrade": false}
System Response: Error: Module vpc — no versions match ~> 4.0 in registry app.terraform.io/acme/vpc/aws; available: 5.10.0, 5.21.0
Recovery reasoning: The module now resolves from the private acme registry, which only published 5.x; the 4.x line was never migrated. Check the 5.21.0 changelog for breaking input changes before bumping, so VPC behavior doesn't silently change.
<|call:read_module_source|> {"module": "acme/vpc/aws", "version": "5.21.0", "show": "changelog,input_diff_from_4.0"}
System Response: 5.x renames input enable_nat to enable_nat_gateway; otherwise compatible; our module call already uses enable_nat_gateway
Recovery reasoning: The only breaking rename is already adopted in our call, so 5.21.0 is safe for us. Update the version constraint, then re-init with upgrade.
<|call:edit_tf_config|> {"path": "eks/main.tf", "patch": {"module.vpc.version": "~> 5.21"}}
System Response: module vpc constraint updated to ~> 5.21
Recovery reasoning: Constraint set; run init with upgrade to pull the module from the private registry.
<|call:run_terraform_init|> {"upgrade": true}
System Response: Downloaded acme/vpc/aws 5.21.0; init successful; plan shows 0 infra changes since the input mapping is unchanged
Resolution: Root cause was the vpc module moving to a private registry that only publishes 5.x, leaving ~> 4.0 unresolvable; after verifying the sole breaking input rename was already adopted, bumping the constraint to ~> 5.21 restored init with zero infra change.

Reasoning: The plan-time error says for_each keys depend on apply-time attributes — the cert's validation options don't exist yet. Reproduce the plan to capture the exact unknown source.
<|call:run_terraform_plan|> {"workspace": "prod"}
System Response: Error: Invalid for_each argument — the for_each value depends on resource attributes that cannot be determined until apply (source aws_acm_certificate.api.domain_validation_options)
Recovery reasoning: The for_each keys derive from a certificate created in the same apply. Read the expression to confirm the dependency.
<|call:read_tf_config|> {"path": "dns/records.tf", "match": "for_each"}
System Response: for_each iterates aws_acm_certificate.api.domain_validation_options keyed by domain_name — the cert is created in this same apply, so keys are unknown at plan
Recovery reasoning: Create the certificate alone first so its validation options become known, then the dependent records can expand. Apply just the cert with -target.
<|call:run_terraform_apply|> {"target": "aws_acm_certificate.api", "auto_approve": false}
System Response: aws_acm_certificate.api created; 3 domain_validation_options now known; status PENDING_VALIDATION
Recovery reasoning: The validation options are now concrete, so for_each keys are determinable. Run the full apply to create the records.
<|call:run_terraform_apply|> {"workspace": "prod", "auto_approve": false}
System Response: Plan: 3 to add (route53 validation records); applied; certificate moved to ISSUED; dns stack converged
Resolution: Root cause was a for_each whose keys derived from an as-yet-uncreated ACM certificate, making them unknown at plan; applying the certificate first with -target made the validation options concrete so the dependent records expanded and applied cleanly.

Reasoning: The destroy is blocked by a prevent_destroy guard on this bucket. I won't globally disable it; I reproduce the destroy to confirm the guard is the blocker and that it's scoped to this single resource.
<|call:run_terraform_apply|> {"target": "aws_s3_bucket.audit_logs_old", "destroy": true}
System Response: Error: Instance cannot be destroyed — resource aws_s3_bucket.audit_logs_old has lifecycle.prevent_destroy set to true
Recovery reasoning: Before lifting the guard I must verify the data was actually migrated, or I could delete the only copy. Check the bucket is empty and replication completed.
<|call:describe_cloud_resource|> {"service": "s3", "id": "audit-logs-old", "field": "ObjectCount,ReplicationStatus"}
System Response: ObjectCount 0; ReplicationStatus COMPLETED to audit-logs-2026 — migration verified, bucket empty
Recovery reasoning: Data is fully migrated and the bucket is empty, so destroying it is safe. Flip prevent_destroy to false on just this resource, leaving every other bucket's guard intact.
<|call:edit_tf_config|> {"path": "storage/buckets.tf", "patch": {"aws_s3_bucket.audit_logs_old.lifecycle.prevent_destroy": false}}
System Response: prevent_destroy set false for audit_logs_old only; other buckets' guards untouched
Recovery reasoning: The guard is cleared for this bucket alone; run the targeted destroy.
<|call:run_terraform_apply|> {"target": "aws_s3_bucket.audit_logs_old", "destroy": true, "auto_approve": false}
System Response: Plan: 1 to destroy; aws_s3_bucket.audit_logs_old removed; all other buckets unchanged
Resolution: Root cause was a deliberate prevent_destroy guard on the legacy bucket; after verifying replication completed and the bucket was empty, disabling the guard for that single resource allowed a clean targeted destroy while every other bucket's guard stayed intact.

Reasoning: A cycle between the app and db security groups means each one's inline ingress references the other's id. Reproduce the plan to capture the exact cycle members.
<|call:run_terraform_plan|> {"workspace": "prod"}
System Response: Error: Cycle: aws_security_group.app, aws_security_group.db
Recovery reasoning: Confirm how the references are wired by rendering the dependency graph around the two groups.
<|call:terraform_graph|> {"focus": "aws_security_group.app,aws_security_group.db", "show": "cycle"}
System Response: Cycle app -> db -> app via inline ingress: app.ingress.security_groups=[db.id] and db.ingress.security_groups=[app.id]
Recovery reasoning: Inline mutual ingress makes neither group creatable first. Extract both cross-references into standalone aws_security_group_rule resources, which breaks the inline coupling while keeping the same allowed traffic.
<|call:edit_tf_config|> {"path": "platform/sg.tf", "patch": {"extract_inline_ingress_to": ["aws_security_group_rule.app_from_db", "aws_security_group_rule.db_from_app"]}}
System Response: inline ingress removed; two standalone aws_security_group_rule resources added referencing each group id
Recovery reasoning: The groups no longer reference each other directly, so re-plan should resolve without a cycle while preserving the rules.
<|call:run_terraform_plan|> {"workspace": "prod"}
System Response: Plan: 2 to add (security_group_rule.app_from_db, db_from_app), 0 to destroy; no cycle; mutual access preserved
Resolution: Root cause was mutual inline ingress rules creating a dependency cycle between the app and db security groups; extracting the cross-references into standalone aws_security_group_rule resources broke the cycle while preserving the same bidirectional access.

Reasoning: A DAG that is unpaused and green but never creates a run, while every other DAG schedules, is almost never a scheduler fault - it is a start_date/catchup interaction. Probe its schedule state first.
<|call:describe_dag_schedule|> {"dag_id": "daily_revenue_rollup"}
System Response: scheduler healthy; is_paused=false; is_active=true; schedule_interval=0 2 * * *; last_parsed=ok; next_dagrun=null
Recovery reasoning: Everything reads healthy except next_dagrun=null - the scheduler cannot compute an interval to fire. That points squarely at start_date, not the scheduler. Inspect the date bounds.
<|call:inspect_dag_config|> {"dag_id": "daily_revenue_rollup", "fields": "start_date,catchup,end_date"}
System Response: start_date=2026-06-29T00:00:00+00:00 (in the future); catchup=False; end_date=None
Recovery reasoning: start_date is two days in the future, so no schedule interval has elapsed yet; with catchup=False it will only fire after the first full interval past start_date. Pin a static past start_date - never a dynamic datetime.now().
<|call:patch_dag_config|> {"dag_id": "daily_revenue_rollup", "patch": {"start_date": "2026-06-20T00:00:00+00:00"}, "redeploy": true}
System Response: dag reparsed; next_dagrun=2026-06-27T02:00:00+00:00 scheduled; run created and queued
Resolution: Root cause was a future start_date so no schedule interval had elapsed; pinned start_date to a static past date while keeping catchup=False so we do not stampede historical runs.

Reasoning: Queued tasks with idle workers means the scheduler will not dispatch - that is a pool with zero free slots, not a worker shortage. Check the default pool the DAG presumably uses.
<|call:check_pool_slots|> {"pool": "default_pool"}
System Response: default_pool: 128 slots, 3 used, 125 open
Recovery reasoning: default_pool has 125 open slots, so these tasks are not blocked there - they must target a custom pool. List the queued instances and their assigned pool.
<|call:list_task_instances|> {"dag_id": "warehouse_sync", "state": "queued", "fields": "task_id,pool,priority_weight"}
System Response: 12 tasks -> pool=etl_heavy, priority_weight=1
Recovery reasoning: They are on etl_heavy, not default. The new fraud DAG likely consumed etl_heavy with a higher priority_weight. Inspect that pool's occupancy.
<|call:check_pool_slots|> {"pool": "etl_heavy"}
System Response: etl_heavy: 4 slots, 4 used (all held by dag=urgent_fraud_scan priority_weight=10), 0 open
Recovery reasoning: urgent_fraud_scan with priority_weight=10 grabbed all 4 etl_heavy slots and is long-running, starving warehouse_sync. Expand the pool rather than killing the in-flight fraud scan.
<|call:set_pool_slots|> {"pool": "etl_heavy", "slots": 8}
System Response: etl_heavy now 8 slots; 4 warehouse_sync tasks immediately dispatched to running
Resolution: Root cause was pool starvation - a new priority_weight=10 DAG monopolized the 4-slot etl_heavy pool; expanded it to 8 slots so both DAGs run without preempting the running fraud scan.

Reasoning: A flooded scheduler log with heartbeat lag could be the scheduler itself overloaded. Tail the scheduler log to see what is generating the volume before blaming any one task.
<|call:get_scheduler_log|> {"dag_id": "hourly_ingest", "tail": 50}
System Response: scheduler process healthy, CPU 31%, no errors; log is 99% repeated INFO 'Poking for s3://lake/ingest/{{ ds }}/_SUCCESS' from s3_partition_sensor
Recovery reasoning: The scheduler is not faulted - it is drowning in pokes from one sensor. That is a poke-mode/interval problem, not capacity. Inspect the sensor config.
<|call:get_sensor_state|> {"dag_id": "hourly_ingest", "task_id": "s3_partition_sensor"}
System Response: mode=poke; poke_interval=1; timeout=21600; soft_fail=false; holds a worker slot for the full window
Recovery reasoning: poke_interval=1s in mode=poke pins a worker the whole time and logs every second - that is the flood. Before widening the interval, confirm the file lands within a safe window.
<|call:query_source_db|> {"source": "s3", "query": "SELECT max(landing_delay_minutes), percentile(landing_delay_minutes,0.99) FROM ingest_audit WHERE last_7d"}
System Response: p99 landing_delay=95min; max=140min
Recovery reasoning: Files land within ~95min p99, so a reschedule-mode sensor at a 5-min interval with a 4h timeout is safe and frees the worker between checks. Reconfigure.
<|call:patch_dag_config|> {"dag_id": "hourly_ingest", "patch": {"task": "s3_partition_sensor", "mode": "reschedule", "poke_interval": 300, "timeout": 14400}, "redeploy": true}
System Response: sensor switched to reschedule; worker freed between pokes; scheduler heartbeat back to <2s; log volume down 99%
Resolution: Root cause was a poke-mode sensor at poke_interval=1s flooding logs and pinning a slot; switched to reschedule mode at 5-min intervals (timeout > observed p99 delay) so the worker frees and the scheduler stops lagging.

Reasoning: 540 concurrent backfill runs saturating Postgres connections - I need to throttle the backfill without killing prod. First enumerate the running runs.
<|call:get_dag_run_state|> {"dag_id": "customer_etl", "state": "running"}
System Response: ERROR: query timed out after 30s - psycopg2 OperationalError: FATAL: remaining connection slots reserved for superuser
Recovery reasoning: The trap is the DB is so saturated I cannot even query it - which confirms the connection storm. I must shed load before I can manage anything. Cap max_active_runs so no new backfill runs spawn.
<|call:patch_dag_config|> {"dag_id": "customer_etl", "patch": {"max_active_runs": 4, "max_active_tasks": 16}, "redeploy": true}
System Response: config applied; scheduler will not exceed 4 concurrent runs; 536 excess runs returning to queued
Recovery reasoning: New cap stops fresh runs; now bound the workers draining the already-running excess via a dedicated backfill pool so Postgres connections recover and prod can breathe.
<|call:set_pool_slots|> {"pool": "backfill_pool", "slots": 8}
System Response: backfill_pool capped at 8; metadata DB connections falling 510 -> 90; prod ETL tasks resuming
Resolution: Root cause was an unbounded backfill spawning 540 concurrent runs and exhausting the Postgres connection pool; capped max_active_runs=4 plus a dedicated 8-slot backfill_pool so history backfills in controlled waves without starving prod.

Reasoning: transform writing 0 rows right after a step that XComs a full dataframe smells like the downstream pulled None. Check the transform task log to see what it actually received.
<|call:get_task_log|> {"dag_id": "feature_pipeline", "task_id": "transform", "try_number": 1}
System Response: transform ran 0.3s, exit 0, state=SUCCESS, 'pulled xcom return_value=None -> wrote 0 rows'
Recovery reasoning: Misleading success: transform is green but pulled None, so the push side failed silently. The error is on extract's XCom write, not transform. Read the actual XCom value extract stored.
<|call:get_xcom_value|> {"dag_id": "feature_pipeline", "task_id": "extract", "key": "return_value"}
System Response: ERROR writing XCom earlier: psycopg2 StringDataRightTruncation - serialized value 48MB exceeds metadata xcom.value limit; row not committed (so pull returns None)
Recovery reasoning: extract's 48MB dataframe XCom was rejected by the metadata DB, so transform pulled None. Do not push the dataframe through XCom - stage it to object storage and pass only a URI.
<|call:patch_dag_config|> {"dag_id": "feature_pipeline", "patch": {"task": "extract", "xcom_backend": "s3", "push": "uri_only"}, "redeploy": true}
System Response: extract now writes parquet to s3://staging/feature_pipeline/{run_id}.parquet and XComs the 142-byte URI; transform reads 1.2M rows; pipeline green
Resolution: Root cause was a 48MB dataframe pushed through XCom exceeding the metadata DB value limit, so the write was rejected and transform silently pulled None; moved the payload to S3 and XCom only the URI, restoring full row counts.

Reasoning: A hard cap of 32 with free pool slots and 128 worker capacity is a parallelism/concurrency config ceiling, not workers or pools. First rule out the workers themselves.
<|call:check_executor_status|> {}
System Response: CeleryExecutor healthy; 8 workers online; 128 worker slots available; queue depth 400; 0 worker errors
Recovery reasoning: Workers are healthy with 128-slot headroom, so the cap is upstream of dispatch - the scheduler's global parallelism. Inspect core config.
<|call:inspect_variable|> {"scope": "config", "keys": "core.parallelism,core.max_active_tasks_per_dag,scheduler.max_tis_per_query"}
System Response: core.parallelism=32; core.max_active_tasks_per_dag=16; scheduler.max_tis_per_query=512
Recovery reasoning: core.parallelism=32 caps cluster-wide running tasks below the 128-slot fleet - the default carried through the 2.x upgrade. Raise it to match capacity, keeping per-DAG concurrency bounded so one DAG cannot hog.
<|call:patch_dag_config|> {"scope": "config", "patch": {"core.parallelism": 128, "core.max_active_tasks_per_dag": 32}, "restart": "scheduler"}
System Response: parallelism=128 applied; scheduler restarted; running tasks ramped 32 -> 118; queue draining
Resolution: Root cause was core.parallelism=32 (a default carried through the 2.x upgrade) capping cluster-wide running tasks below the 128-slot worker fleet; raised parallelism to 128 to match capacity while bounding per-DAG concurrency.

Reasoning: A load failing on a missing column right after an upstream 'cleanup' is schema drift - a column renamed or dropped. First confirm the source vs the DAG's expected schema.
<|call:validate_schema|> {"source": "orders", "against": "orders_load.expected_schema"}
System Response: schema validation PASSED - 24/24 expected columns present; checksum match
Recovery reasoning: The validator says pass but the live load fails on discount_pct - the validator is reading a cached schema snapshot, not the live source. Query the live information_schema directly.
<|call:query_source_db|> {"source": "orders", "query": "SELECT column_name FROM information_schema.columns WHERE table_name='orders'"}
System Response: order_id, ts, amount, discount_amount, discount_rate, ... (no discount_pct) - discount_pct was renamed to discount_rate
Recovery reasoning: Upstream renamed discount_pct -> discount_rate. Do not hardcode the rename blindly; add an explicit map plus a guard so future drift fails loudly at extract instead of silently.
<|call:patch_dag_config|> {"dag_id": "orders_load", "patch": {"column_map": {"discount_pct": "discount_rate"}, "fail_on_unmapped": true}, "redeploy": true}
System Response: mapping applied; 03:00 run cleared and reran -> load succeeds, 1.4M rows; unmapped-column guard active
Resolution: Root cause was upstream renaming discount_pct to discount_rate (schema drift) that a stale schema-cache validator missed; added an explicit column map plus a fail_on_unmapped guard so future drift surfaces at extract.

Reasoning: Doubled totals after a rerun means a non-idempotent append load. First read the suspect partition's counts before changing any write logic.
<|call:query_source_db|> {"source": "warehouse", "query": "SELECT count(*), sum(amount) FROM sales_daily WHERE ds='2026-06-25'"}
System Response: count=240000, sum=1482330.50
Recovery reasoning: 240k looks like a plausible single day on its own - I need a baseline. Compare against neighboring days to confirm it is actually doubled rather than a high-volume day.
<|call:query_source_db|> {"source": "warehouse", "query": "SELECT ds, count(*) FROM sales_daily WHERE ds BETWEEN '2026-06-23' AND '2026-06-26' GROUP BY ds ORDER BY ds"}
System Response: 06-23=119k, 06-24=121k, 06-25=240k, 06-26=118k - 06-25 is ~2x its neighbors: duplicate insert confirmed
Recovery reasoning: Confirmed a double-load on rerun. Make the load idempotent with partition-scoped delete-then-insert, then reload the affected partition once to correct it.
<|call:patch_dag_config|> {"dag_id": "daily_sales_agg", "patch": {"task": "load", "write_mode": "delete_insert", "partition_key": "ds"}, "redeploy": true, "rerun_ds": "2026-06-25"}
System Response: load switched to delete+insert on ds; 06-25 partition deleted then reloaded -> count=120k, sum=741165.25; totals correct
Resolution: Root cause was a non-idempotent append-mode load that double-counted 06-25 on rerun; switched to partition-scoped delete-insert so reruns are idempotent, then reloaded the partition to halve it back.

Reasoning: A 2h offset that appeared exactly at the DST boundary means the schedule is in UTC (or a naive cron) rather than timezone-aware. Inspect the DAG's timezone and schedule.
<|call:describe_dag_schedule|> {"dag_id": "eu_report"}
System Response: schedule_interval='0 9 * * *'; next_run=2026-06-28T09:00:00; timezone=UTC
Recovery reasoning: The '0 9' looks correct, but timezone=UTC means 09:00 UTC = 11:00 CEST after DST. Before patching, confirm the deployment default so I fix this at the right level.
<|call:inspect_variable|> {"scope": "config", "keys": "core.default_timezone"}
System Response: core.default_timezone=utc
Recovery reasoning: The global default is UTC and the DAG never overrode it. Pin a timezone-aware pendulum schedule on this DAG so it tracks DST automatically instead of a fixed UTC offset that breaks twice a year.
<|call:patch_dag_config|> {"dag_id": "eu_report", "patch": {"timezone": "Europe/Berlin", "schedule_interval": "0 9 * * *"}, "redeploy": true}
System Response: dag tz=Europe/Berlin; next_run=2026-06-28T09:00:00+02:00 (07:00 UTC); fires 09:00 local across DST
Resolution: Root cause was a UTC-scheduled DAG so 09:00 UTC drifted to 11:00 Berlin after the March DST shift; pinned a pendulum Europe/Berlin timezone so the 09:00 local schedule follows DST automatically.

Reasoning: 'Detected as zombie' while the worker is still busy means the task stopped heartbeating - likely a long blocking step or a memory blowup, not application logic. Read the task log around the kill.
<|call:get_task_log|> {"dag_id": "clickstream_etl", "task_id": "dedupe", "try_number": 2}
System Response: log ends abruptly mid 'sorting 80M rows in pandas' with no traceback, then scheduler SIGKILL; no OOMKilled line emitted
Recovery reasoning: No exception and no OOM line looks like a silent crash, but the process was alive and just stopped reporting - that is a heartbeat gap. Check worker memory/CPU before touching any threshold.
<|call:get_worker_metrics|> {"dag_id": "clickstream_etl", "task_id": "dedupe", "window": "10m"}
System Response: RSS climbed to 28GB on a 32GB worker; heavy swap si/so; load avg 40; process alive but starved, heartbeat stalled >300s
Recovery reasoning: The 80M-row in-memory pandas sort thrashed swap and stalled the heartbeat past scheduler_zombie_task_threshold (300s), so it was reaped. Fix the real cause - memory - with a chunked external sort, and add threshold margin.
<|call:patch_dag_config|> {"dag_id": "clickstream_etl", "patch": {"task": "dedupe", "execution": "chunked_external_sort", "chunk_rows": 5000000, "zombie_threshold_s": 600}, "redeploy": true}
System Response: dedupe now external-sorts in 5M-row chunks; peak RSS 28GB -> 9GB; heartbeats steady; 0 zombie kills over 20 runs
Resolution: Root cause was an 80M-row in-memory sort thrashing swap and stalling heartbeats past the 300s zombie threshold; switched to a chunked external sort and raised the threshold to 600s as margin.

Reasoning: Deferred tasks stuck cluster-wide while normal tasks run means the triggerer (which resumes deferred tasks) is down or wedged - not any single DAG. Check its status.
<|call:check_triggerer_status|> {}
System Response: triggerer service: RUNNING; 1 instance; last_heartbeat=2026-06-27T02:01:13Z
Recovery reasoning: It reports RUNNING but the heartbeat is frozen at 02:01 - a hung process, not a stopped one. Read its log to see what wedged the loop.
<|call:get_scheduler_log|> {"component": "triggerer", "tail": 30}
System Response: repeated 'asyncio event loop blocked >120s', then no lines after 02:01; one custom async sensor raised an unhandled exception that killed the loop
Recovery reasoning: An unhandled exception in a custom async sensor killed the triggerer's single event loop, freezing all triggers. Restart it to resume the deferred backlog, and flag the sensor to guard its exceptions.
<|call:restart_scheduler|> {"component": "triggerer", "drain": false}
System Response: triggerer restarted; heartbeat live; 312 deferred tasks resumed and moved to running/success within 90s
Resolution: Root cause was a hung triggerer - an unhandled exception in a custom async sensor killed its event loop and stranded every deferred task; restarted the triggerer to drain the backlog and flagged the sensor for exception-guarding so one trigger cannot wedge the shared loop.

Reasoning: 1,200 SLA-miss pages for tasks that succeeded means SLA is being evaluated against the wrong reference time, not real lateness. First confirm the tasks truly met wall-clock SLA.
<|call:get_sla_misses|> {"dag_id": "finance_close", "date": "2026-06-26"}
System Response: 0 actual late completions; every task finished within its duration; yet 1200 sla_miss rows recorded
Recovery reasoning: Completions were on time but miss rows still generated - the SLA is computed wrong. SLA is measured from the run's data_interval_start; a recent schedule change likely widened that gap. Inspect SLA defs vs schedule.
<|call:inspect_dag_config|> {"dag_id": "finance_close", "fields": "schedule_interval,tasks.sla"}
System Response: schedule_interval changed weekly->daily last week; task sla=timedelta(hours=20) unchanged; SLA is relative to data_interval_start, now 24h earlier, so every task 'misses' the 20h window despite running fine
Recovery reasoning: The weekly->daily switch left 20h SLAs that now always elapse before each daily run finishes, firing phantom misses. Resize SLAs to the daily cadence and de-dupe the callback so it pages once per run, not per task.
<|call:patch_dag_config|> {"dag_id": "finance_close", "patch": {"tasks.sla": "timedelta(hours=3)", "sla_miss_callback": "dedupe_per_run"}, "redeploy": true}
System Response: SLAs resized to 3h matching the daily cadence; callback dedup enabled; next run records 0 sla_miss; paging back to baseline
Resolution: Root cause was 20h task SLAs left over from a weekly schedule that always elapse under the new daily cadence, firing phantom misses; resized SLAs to 3h and de-duped the callback so one run pages at most once.

Reasoning: 48k mapped instances from one expand() means the upstream feeding the mapping returned a far larger list than expected - bad input, not a mapping bug. Inspect the XCom that fed the expand.
<|call:get_xcom_value|> {"dag_id": "vendor_fanout", "task_id": "list_vendor_files", "key": "return_value"}
System Response: return_value type=list, length=48000
Recovery reasoning: 48k looks plausibly real until I check cardinality - we have ~300 vendors, not 48k. The list is likely all files across history, not today's partition. Verify against the source.
<|call:query_source_db|> {"source": "vendor_meta", "query": "SELECT count(DISTINCT vendor_id) FROM vendors; SELECT count(*) FROM vendor_files"}
System Response: distinct vendors=312; vendor_files=48000 (every file across all history, not just today's partition)
Recovery reasoning: The mapping fanned out over all 48k historical files instead of the ~312 for today's partition. Scope the upstream query to the run's data interval and cap max_map_length so a bad input cannot explode the scheduler again.
<|call:patch_dag_config|> {"dag_id": "vendor_fanout", "patch": {"task": "list_vendor_files", "filter": "ds = {{ ds }}", "max_map_length": 1024}, "redeploy": true}
System Response: list now returns 312 files for the partition; expand creates 312 mapped tasks; UI grid loads; scheduler expand time 38s -> 0.4s
Resolution: Root cause was the mapping source querying all 48k historical files instead of the current partition, exploding into 48k mapped tasks; scoped the query to {{ ds }} and set max_map_length=1024 as a guardrail against future input blowups.

Reasoning: Tasks failing on log write with ENOSPC on specific workers is a full disk, usually unrotated task logs. Check the logs path on the affected workers first.
<|call:check_disk_usage|> {"hosts": ["worker-3", "worker-5", "worker-7"], "path": "/opt/airflow/logs"}
System Response: worker-3 /opt/airflow/logs = 41% used (180GB free); same on worker-5/7
Recovery reasoning: The logs path has plenty free, so ENOSPC is coming from a different mount on these hosts - likely the root fs where temp spills go. Check all mounts on one failing worker.
<|call:check_disk_usage|> {"hosts": ["worker-3"], "path": "ALL"}
System Response: / = 100% (0 bytes free); /tmp lives on root fs and holds 230GB of orphaned pandas .to_parquet spill files from previously killed tasks; /opt/airflow/logs is a separate volume at 41%
